// The written history for each map: era title, overview, India, and why each change happened.
export const STORIES = [{"year":-123000,"title":"A world of many human species","overview":"During a warm spell between ice ages, several kinds of humans shared the planet. Our own species lived in Africa, Neanderthals roamed Europe and western Asia, and older groups such as Homo erectus survived in parts of Asia. Nobody farmed; everyone hunted and gathered.","india":"Toolmakers had lived in India for a very long time: hand axes at Attirampakkam near Chennai are over a million years old. Which human species lived here at this moment is unknown, because very few fossils survive.","changes":[],"spotlight":[{"name":"Neanderthal","fact":"Neanderthals buried their dead, made fire and used pigments, and most people outside Africa today still carry a little Neanderthal DNA."},{"name":"Homo erectus","fact":"Homo erectus was the first human species known to leave Africa, and it lasted well over a million years, far longer than our species has existed so far."}]},{"year":-10000,"title":"The Ice Age ends","overview":"The last Ice Age was ending, sea levels were rising and modern humans had spread to every continent except Antarctica. All other human species had died out. In the Levant, people were starting to settle down and would soon farm wheat and barley.","india":"India was home to Mesolithic hunter-gatherers using tiny stone blades called microliths. Rock shelters like Bhimbetka in Madhya Pradesh hold paintings made by such communities over many thousands of years.","changes":[{"name":"Homo heidelbergensis","kind":"vanished","why":"This older human species had died out long before; it is thought to be an ancestor of both Neanderthals and our own species."},{"name":"Homo erectus","kind":"vanished","why":"Homo erectus died out in its last refuges in Southeast Asia, with some of the latest fossils from Java dating to over 100,000 years ago."},{"name":"Neanderthal","kind":"vanished","why":"Neanderthals disappeared around 40,000 years ago, not long after modern humans spread into Europe. Interbreeding means part of their DNA lives on in us."},{"name":"Aboriginal tribes","kind":"appeared","why":"Modern humans had reached Australia at least 50,000 years ago by sea, and Aboriginal Australians have lived there ever since."},{"name":"Khoisan","kind":"appeared","why":"The Khoisan peoples of southern Africa descend from some of the most ancient human lineages, with a deep history of hunting and gathering."},{"name":"Austronesians","kind":"appeared","why":"The map labels the peoples of island Southeast Asia with this name. The famous Austronesian seafaring expansion out of Taiwan came much later, from around 3000 BC."},{"name":"Steppe Mesolithic Hunter-Foragers","kind":"appeared","why":"As the ice retreated, hunter-foragers spread across the warming grasslands of the Eurasian steppe."},{"name":"Coastal and Woodland Mesolithic Hunter-Foragers","kind":"appeared","why":"Forests spread across Europe after the Ice Age, and people adapted by hunting deer, fishing and gathering along coasts and rivers."},{"name":"Jōmon","kind":"appeared","why":"The Jōmon people of Japan made some of the world's oldest pottery, dating back to around 14,000 BC, while still living as foragers."}],"spotlight":[{"name":"Levantine Corridor (Neolithic Farmers)","fact":"This strip of the Middle East was one of the first places on Earth where people began to cultivate plants and live in permanent villages."},{"name":"Aboriginal Tasmanians","fact":"Rising seas were about to cut Tasmania off from mainland Australia, leaving its people isolated for around 10,000 years."}]},{"year":-8000,"title":"First farmers","overview":"The climate had warmed into the Holocene, the stable era we still live in. Farming was taking hold in the Fertile Crescent, with sheep and goats being domesticated, while most of the world still lived by hunting, fishing and gathering.","india":"Within about a thousand years, people at Mehrgarh in Balochistan would be growing barley and wheat and herding goats, the earliest known farming village in South Asia. Elsewhere, microlith-using hunter-gatherers lived across India.","changes":[{"name":"Bantu","kind":"appeared","why":"The map shows the Bantu-speaking peoples early. Their homeland was near today's Nigeria-Cameroon border; their great migration across central and southern Africa came thousands of years later."}],"spotlight":[{"name":"Jōmon","fact":"Jōmon people lived in settled villages and made elaborate pottery for thousands of years without becoming full-time farmers."},{"name":"Okhotsk culture","fact":"This name belongs to a much later sea-mammal hunting culture of the Sea of Okhotsk; the map uses it for the far northeast of Asia."}]},{"year":-5000,"title":"Farming spreads across Eurasia","overview":"Farming had spread from the Middle East into Europe, North Africa and Central Asia. In Mesopotamia the Ubaid culture was building villages and temples that would grow into the first cities. Many regional cultures, named by archaeologists after their pottery, appear on the map.","india":"Mehrgarh had grown into a busy farming village with pottery and beads; a copper bead there preserved some of the world's oldest known cotton threads, around 6000 BC. Farming was slowly spreading across the northwest.","changes":[{"name":"Cardial Ware culture","kind":"appeared","why":"Early farmers spread along Mediterranean coasts by boat, decorating their pottery with the edges of cockle shells, which gives the culture its name."},{"name":"Kelteminar culture","kind":"appeared","why":"Neolithic hunters and fishers lived around the Aral Sea and the deserts of Central Asia, known for their distinctive pottery."},{"name":"Funnel-Beaker","kind":"appeared","why":"Named after its pots with funnel-shaped necks, this was one of northern Europe's first farming cultures, and its people built large stone tombs."},{"name":"Dravidians","kind":"appeared","why":"The map places early Dravidian-speaking peoples in South Asia. When and where the Dravidian languages began is still debated by scholars."},{"name":"Norte Chico","kind":"appeared","why":"The coast of Peru would host Norte Chico, the oldest known civilization in the Americas, with monumental towns like Caral built from around 3000 BC."},{"name":"Early combware","kind":"appeared","why":"Foragers in the Baltic and Finland made pottery decorated with comb-like stamps, giving this culture its name."},{"name":"Narva","kind":"appeared","why":"The Narva culture of the eastern Baltic made pottery while still living mainly by fishing, hunting and gathering."}],"spotlight":[{"name":"Ubaid","fact":"Ubaid villagers in southern Iraq built some of the first temples on raised platforms, at sites like Eridu."},{"name":"Valdivia","fact":"The Valdivia culture of coastal Ecuador made some of the oldest pottery in the Americas, along with small stone and clay figurines."},{"name":"Dimini","fact":"Dimini in Greece was a Neolithic settlement ringed by several low stone walls, one of the best preserved in Europe."}]},{"year":-4000,"title":"On the eve of cities","overview":"In Mesopotamia, the Uruk period was beginning: towns were swelling into the world's first cities, and writing would be invented within a few centuries. Along the Nile, farming communities were growing richer, and copper was coming into wider use.","india":"In the Indus region, farming villages were spreading along the rivers. Over the next centuries they grew into the Early Harappan towns that led to one of the world's great Bronze Age civilizations.","changes":[{"name":"Semites","kind":"appeared","why":"The map labels the Semitic-speaking peoples of Arabia and the Levant, the ancestors of Akkadians, Arameans, Hebrews and Arabs."},{"name":"Indus valley civilization","kind":"appeared","why":"The map shows the Indus region as a single civilization early. Its villages would grow into towns from around 3300 BC, and great cities like Harappa from about 2600 BC."},{"name":"Ur","kind":"appeared","why":"Ur in southern Mesopotamia was a growing Sumerian town; Sumer's cities would invent cuneiform writing around 3200 BC."},{"name":"Egypt","kind":"appeared","why":"Predynastic farming cultures along the Nile were becoming more complex. Egypt would be united as one kingdom around 3100 BC."},{"name":"Elam","kind":"grew","why":"Elam, in southwest Iran, centred on Susa, which was founded around 4200 BC and became one of the oldest cities in the world."}],"spotlight":[{"name":"Cycladic","fact":"The Cycladic islanders of the Aegean later became famous for elegant, flat marble figurines that look strikingly modern."},{"name":"Minoan","fact":"Crete was already farmed; its Minoan people would go on to build the great palace of Knossos."}]},{"year":-3000,"title":"Writing and kings","overview":"The world's first written records were appearing in Sumer and Egypt, where kings now ruled a united Nile valley. On the steppe, herders with wagons were on the move, and in Britain the first phase of Stonehenge was being built.","india":"The Early Harappan phase was flourishing, with towns such as Kot Diji and Kalibangan. Within a few centuries these would grow into the planned cities of the Indus civilization, with brick houses and drains.","changes":[{"name":"Yamnaya culture","kind":"appeared","why":"Yamnaya herders of the Pontic-Caspian steppe used wagons and horses. Their descendants spread widely, likely carrying Indo-European languages into Europe and Asia."},{"name":"Afanasevo","kind":"appeared","why":"The Afanasievo culture of the Altai Mountains was closely related to the Yamnaya, and may include ancestors of the Tocharian speakers of later Xinjiang."}],"spotlight":[{"name":"Egypt","fact":"Around 3100 BC Upper and Lower Egypt were united, traditionally by a king called Narmer, starting nearly 3,000 years of pharaohs."},{"name":"Ur","fact":"Sumerian cities like Ur used cuneiform, wedge-shaped marks pressed into clay, first for keeping accounts."},{"name":"Norte Chico","fact":"Caral in Peru, a Norte Chico site, had large pyramids and plazas at about the same time as Egypt's pyramids."}]},{"year":-2000,"title":"The Bronze Age world","overview":"Bronze Age kingdoms were flourishing in Egypt, Mesopotamia, the Indus and China, and Minoan palaces rose on Crete. From here the map is far more detailed, so large regions now carry names describing how people lived, from rice farmers to Arctic hunters.","india":"The great Harappan cities, Mohenjo-daro, Harappa, Dholavira and Lothal, were at the end of their peak. Soon after 1900 BC many were gradually abandoned, probably as rivers shifted and the monsoon weakened.","changes":[{"name":"Australian aboriginal hunter-gatherers","kind":"renamed","why":"Same state, renamed in the map data."},{"name":"Archaic Amerindian hunter-gatherers","kind":"appeared","why":"People had lived across the Americas since at least 15,000 years ago; the map now shows them as hunter-gatherer bands spread across both continents."},{"name":"Saharan pastoral nomads","kind":"appeared","why":"The once-green Sahara had dried out after about 3000 BC, and herders with cattle, sheep and goats moved between oases and pastures."},{"name":"Paleo-Siberian hunter-gatherers","kind":"appeared","why":"The vast forests and tundra of Siberia were home to small groups of hunters and fishers, now shown on the map."},{"name":"Austro-Asiatic rice cultures","kind":"appeared","why":"Rice farming, first developed along the Yangtze, spread into mainland Southeast Asia, carried in part by Austroasiatic-speaking peoples."},{"name":"Arctic marine mammal hunters","kind":"appeared","why":"Paleo-Eskimo peoples had spread across the Arctic from Alaska to Greenland, hunting seals and other sea mammals."},{"name":"Mesoamerican hunter-gatherers and maïze farmers","kind":"appeared","why":"Maize was domesticated in Mexico thousands of years earlier, and farming villages were slowly spreading across Mesoamerica."},{"name":"Andronovo","kind":"appeared","why":"The Andronovo culture spread across the Central Asian steppe. Many scholars link these herders to early Indo-Iranian speakers."},{"name":"Finno-Ugric taiga hunter-gatherers","kind":"appeared","why":"Peoples speaking early Finno-Ugric languages lived by hunting and fishing in the northern forests of Russia."}],"spotlight":[{"name":"Xia","fact":"The Xia is China's first dynasty in traditional histories; archaeologists debate whether the Erlitou culture was its capital."},{"name":"Sintashta","fact":"The Sintashta culture of the southern Urals left some of the earliest known spoked-wheel chariots, buried in graves around 2000 BC."},{"name":"Minoan","fact":"Minoan Crete built the great palace of Knossos, with colourful frescoes and running water."}]},{"year":-1500,"title":"Chariots and empires","overview":"The Late Bronze Age was dominated by Egypt's New Kingdom, the Hittites, Mitanni and Babylon, linked by trade and diplomacy. In China, the Shang dynasty wrote on oracle bones. The map again regroups much of the world by way of life, such as cereal or manioc farming.","india":"With the Harappan cities in decline, Vedic Aryans were settled in the land of seven rivers in the northwest, where the hymns of the Rigveda were composed. Across the rest of India, farming and herding communities kept spreading.","changes":[{"name":"West African cereal farmers","kind":"appeared","why":"Pearl millet and sorghum were domesticated in the Sahel and spread among farming peoples across West Africa."},{"name":"Proto-Altaic pastoralists","kind":"appeared","why":"The map groups the herders of the Mongolian and Manchurian grasslands under this label, an older linguistic theory rather than a known people."},{"name":"Savanna hunter-gatherers","kind":"appeared","why":"Hunter-gatherers still lived across much of the African savanna before Bantu-speaking farmers arrived."},{"name":"Manioc farmers","kind":"appeared","why":"Manioc, or cassava, was domesticated in the southwestern Amazon and became a staple food for farming peoples across tropical South America."},{"name":"Subarctic forest hunter-gatherers","kind":"appeared","why":"The map now gives the hunters of northern Canada's forests their own label, following caribou and fishing the lakes."},{"name":"Karasuk culture","kind":"appeared","why":"The Karasuk culture of southern Siberia were Bronze Age herders, known for their bronze knives with curved handles."}],"spotlight":[{"name":"Egypt","fact":"Hatshepsut, one of the few women to rule as pharaoh, reigned around this time and sent a famous trading expedition to Punt."},{"name":"Sinic","fact":"The Shang kings of China asked their ancestors questions by cracking bones and shells, and these oracle bones hold the earliest Chinese writing."},{"name":"Olmec","fact":"The Olmec of Mexico's Gulf coast are often called the mother culture of Mesoamerica, famous for giant carved stone heads."}]},{"year":-1000,"title":"Iron after the collapse","overview":"Around 1200 BC the Late Bronze Age world collapsed: the Hittite empire fell, cities were burned and the Sea Peoples raided Egypt. Iron spread in the aftermath. In China the Zhou had overthrown the Shang, and the Phoenicians spread their alphabet by sea.","india":"In the Later Vedic period, iron tools were spreading in the Ganga plain, and the Kuru kingdom was a centre of Vedic culture. In the south and the Deccan, Iron Age communities were building megalithic burials.","changes":[],"spotlight":[{"name":"Zhoa","fact":"The Zhou dynasty overthrew the Shang around 1046 BC and justified it with the Mandate of Heaven, an idea used by Chinese rulers for 3,000 years."},{"name":"Kingdom of David and Solomon","fact":"According to the Bible, David and then Solomon ruled a united kingdom of Israel from Jerusalem around 1000 BC."},{"name":"Gandhara grave culture","fact":"In the Swat valley of Pakistan, this culture is known for its graves, and some link it to the early Vedic peoples."}]},{"year":-700,"title":"Assyria and the Iron Age","overview":"The Assyrian Empire was at its height, ruling from Mesopotamia to the Levant under kings like Sennacherib. Greeks were founding colonies around the Mediterranean, Kushite kings ruled Egypt, and horse nomads were riding out across the steppe.","india":"Vedic culture had spread east along the Ganga, where kingdoms like Kuru and Panchala grew. Towns were reappearing in north India, and the early Upanishads were being composed around this time.","changes":[{"name":"Vedic Aryans","kind":"grew","why":"Vedic peoples spread east from Punjab into the Ganga plain, helped by iron tools that made clearing forest for farming easier."},{"name":"Dravidians","kind":"shrank","why":"Shown as shrinking because Vedic culture spread into the Ganga plain and central India. Dravidian-speaking peoples remained the majority in the south."},{"name":"Cimerians","kind":"shrank","why":"The Cimmerians, nomads north of the Black Sea, were pushed out by the Scythians in the 8th to 7th centuries BC and raided into Anatolia."},{"name":"Proto-Scythian culture","kind":"appeared","why":"Early Scythian-type horse nomads emerged on the steppe, with mounted archery and a distinctive animal-style art."},{"name":"Chernoles culture","kind":"appeared","why":"The Chernoles culture of the Ukrainian forest-steppe were farmers, and some scholars see them as possible ancestors of the early Slavs."},{"name":"Lusatian culture","kind":"appeared","why":"The Lusatian culture of Poland and eastern Germany built fortified settlements like Biskupin, with wooden houses on a lake island."},{"name":"Milograd culture","kind":"appeared","why":"The Milograd culture of Belarus and northern Ukraine is sometimes linked to the Neuri people described by the Greek historian Herodotus."}],"spotlight":[{"name":"Assyria","fact":"Assyria built a great library at Nineveh, where King Ashurbanipal later collected thousands of clay tablets, including the Epic of Gilgamesh."},{"name":"Kush","fact":"The Kushite kings of Nubia conquered Egypt and ruled it as its 25th dynasty, building pyramids back home in Sudan."},{"name":"Greek city-states","fact":"The first recorded Olympic Games were held in 776 BC, and Homer's epics were taking shape around this time."}]},{"year":-500,"title":"Persia and the Axial Age","overview":"The Achaemenid Persian Empire was the largest the world had yet seen, from Egypt to the Indus. It was a time of great thinkers: the Buddha and Mahavira in India, Confucius in China and early philosophers in Greece. Rome had just become a republic.","india":"North India was divided into the Mahajanapadas, sixteen great kingdoms and republics such as Magadha, Kosala, Vatsa and Avanti. The Buddha and Mahavira were teaching in the Ganga plain, and Gandhara became a Persian province.","changes":[{"name":"Achaemenid Empire","kind":"appeared","why":"Cyrus the Great conquered the Medes, Lydia and Babylon (539 BC); his successors added Egypt, and Darius I pushed the empire to the Indus."},{"name":"Vedic Aryans","kind":"vanished","why":"The Vedic tribes had settled into territorial kingdoms, now shown as separate Mahajanapadas like Kuru, Pancala, Kosala and Magadha."},{"name":"Iranian pastoralists","kind":"vanished","why":"Iranian peoples like the Medes and Persians had built states, and much of the Iranian plateau was now part of the Achaemenid Empire."},{"name":"Sinic","kind":"vanished","why":"China is now shown as the Zhou states: the Zhou kings had lost real power, and many rival states competed in the Spring and Autumn period."},{"name":"Urnfield cultures","kind":"vanished","why":"Central Europe had moved from the Bronze Age Urnfield cultures into the Iron Age, the world of the early Celts."},{"name":"Karasuk culture","kind":"vanished","why":"The Karasuk Bronze Age culture of southern Siberia had given way to new Iron Age nomadic cultures."}],"spotlight":[{"name":"Magadha","fact":"Magadha, ruled from Rajagriha by kings like Bimbisara and Ajatashatru, was on its way to becoming north India's leading power."},{"name":"Rome","fact":"Rome expelled its last king, traditionally in 509 BC, and became a republic governed by elected officials."},{"name":"Simhala","fact":"Sri Lankan chronicles say Prince Vijaya arrived from India and founded the Sinhala kingdom, traditionally on the day of the Buddha's death."}]},{"year":-400,"title":"Greeks, Persians and nomads","overview":"Persia still ruled a vast empire, while Athens and Sparta had just fought the Peloponnesian War. China was in the Warring States period, and Celtic peoples dominated much of Europe. Out on the steppe, nomadic peoples such as the Scythians controlled huge areas.","india":"Magadha was swallowing its neighbours, including Kosala and the Vajji confederacy, from its new capital at Pataliputra. Soon the Nanda dynasty would make it the largest power in north India.","changes":[{"name":"Hindu kingdoms","kind":"grew","why":"Magadha and other north Indian kingdoms expanded by conquering neighbouring kingdoms and republics, absorbing smaller states."},{"name":"Scythians","kind":"appeared","why":"The Scythians, Iranian-speaking horse archers, ruled the steppe north of the Black Sea and were famously described by Herodotus."},{"name":"Xiongnu","kind":"appeared","why":"The map shows the Mongolian steppe herders as the Xiongnu early. They united as a powerful confederation under Modu Chanyu around 209 BC."},{"name":"Hallstatt culture","kind":"appeared","why":"Hallstatt, named after a salt-mining site in Austria, was the early Iron Age culture of the Celts in central Europe."},{"name":"Zhangzhung Kingdom","kind":"appeared","why":"Zhangzhung was a kingdom in western Tibet, around Mount Kailash, remembered as the cradle of the Bon religion."},{"name":"Proto-Tibetan cultures","kind":"appeared","why":"Herders and farmers of the Tibetan plateau, ancestors of the later Tibetan peoples, are now shown on the map."},{"name":"Proto-Thai cultures","kind":"appeared","why":"Tai-speaking peoples lived in what is now southern China, long before they migrated south into today's Thailand and Laos."}],"spotlight":[{"name":"Roman Republic","fact":"Around 390 BC a Gallic army sacked Rome, a shock that Romans remembered for centuries."},{"name":"Yayoi","fact":"Yayoi people brought wet-rice farming and metalworking to Japan from the Asian mainland."},{"name":"Zapotec","fact":"The Zapotecs of Oaxaca built Monte Albán on a flattened mountaintop and used one of the earliest writing systems in the Americas."}]},{"year":-323,"title":"Alexander's empire","overview":"In just over a decade Alexander the Great of Macedon conquered the whole Persian Empire, from Egypt to Afghanistan and the Punjab. He died in Babylon in 323 BC, aged 32, leaving no clear heir. In China, Qin was rising among the Warring States.","india":"The Nanda dynasty ruled a powerful Magadha across the Ganga plain. Alexander had fought King Porus at the Hydaspes (Jhelum) in 326 BC, but his army refused to march beyond the Beas, and he turned back.","changes":[{"name":"Achaemenid Empire","kind":"vanished","why":"Alexander defeated Darius III at Issus (333 BC) and Gaugamela (331 BC). Darius was killed by his own nobles in 330 BC, ending the Persian Empire."},{"name":"Empire of Alexander","kind":"appeared","why":"Alexander's conquests created an empire from Greece to the Indus in about ten years. It started to break apart as soon as he died in 323 BC."},{"name":"Hindu kingdoms and republics","kind":"appeared","why":"The map now splits north and central India into a separate label for its many kingdoms and republics, alongside Magadha."},{"name":"Hallstatt culture","kind":"vanished","why":"Celtic Europe had moved on to the La Tène culture, famous for its swirling art; the map now simply shows Celts."}],"spotlight":[{"name":"Qin","fact":"The state of Qin, reformed by the strict Legalist minister Shang Yang, would conquer all its rivals and unify China in 221 BC."},{"name":"Carthaginian Empire","fact":"Carthage, founded by Phoenicians, dominated trade in the western Mediterranean from its harbour in today's Tunisia."},{"name":"Magadha","fact":"The Nanda kings were said to be fabulously rich, with an army so large it helped convince Alexander's troops not to go further."}]},{"year":-300,"title":"Successor kingdoms and the Mauryas","overview":"Alexander's generals carved up his empire in decades of war. Seleucus held Asia from Syria to Iran, Ptolemy ruled Egypt, and others fought over Macedon and Anatolia. In India, a new empire rose that was larger than any before it.","india":"Chandragupta Maurya, guided by his adviser Chanakya, overthrew the Nandas and founded the Mauryan Empire, ruling from Pataliputra. The Greek envoy Megasthenes described his capital as huge and wealthy.","changes":[{"name":"Empire of Alexander","kind":"vanished","why":"After Alexander's death his generals, the Diadochi, fought for decades and split the empire into rival kingdoms."},{"name":"Seleucid Kingdom","kind":"appeared","why":"Seleucus I took Babylon and most of Alexander's Asian lands, from Syria to Central Asia."},{"name":"Mauryan Empire","kind":"appeared","why":"Chandragupta Maurya overthrew the Nandas around 321 BC, then took the northwest. He made a treaty with Seleucus, getting land for 500 war elephants."},{"name":"Magadha","kind":"vanished","why":"Magadha did not disappear: it became the heart of the new Mauryan Empire, with Pataliputra as capital."},{"name":"Kingdom of Antigonus","kind":"appeared","why":"Antigonus the One-Eyed held much of Anatolia and Syria, aiming to rule all of Alexander's empire, until he was killed at the Battle of Ipsus in 301 BC."},{"name":"Hindu kingdoms and republics","kind":"shrank","why":"Many independent kingdoms and republics were conquered and absorbed into the expanding Mauryan Empire."},{"name":"Ptolemaic Kingdom","kind":"appeared","why":"Ptolemy I took Egypt and founded a Greek dynasty there; its last ruler would be Cleopatra."},{"name":"Nabatean Kingdom","kind":"appeared","why":"The Nabataeans, Arab traders who controlled incense routes, later carved their capital Petra out of rose-red cliffs in Jordan."}],"spotlight":[{"name":"Ptolemaic Kingdom","fact":"The Ptolemies built the great Library of Alexandria and the Pharos lighthouse, one of the Seven Wonders of the ancient world."},{"name":"Kingdom of Lysimachus","fact":"Lysimachus, another of Alexander's generals, ruled Thrace and later much of Anatolia before dying in battle in 281 BC."},{"name":"Roman Republic","fact":"Rome was fighting the Samnite Wars to take control of central Italy."}]},{"year":-200,"title":"Han, Xiongnu and Rome","overview":"China had been unified by Qin in 221 BC, and after Qin collapsed the Han dynasty took over. To the north the Xiongnu built a steppe empire, and in the west Rome defeated Hannibal. The Mauryan Empire was in decline.","india":"The Mauryan Empire had reached its peak under Ashoka, who turned to Buddhism after the bloody Kalinga war. After his death around 232 BC it weakened, and Indo-Greek kings were about to invade from Bactria.","changes":[{"name":"Han Empire","kind":"appeared","why":"Qin unified China in 221 BC but collapsed after its first emperor died. Liu Bang won the civil war and founded the Han dynasty in 202 BC."},{"name":"Zhou states","kind":"vanished","why":"The last Zhou king was deposed in 256 BC, and Qin conquered all the other Warring States by 221 BC."},{"name":"Xiongnu","kind":"appeared","why":"Modu Chanyu united the Xiongnu around 209 BC and defeated the Han founder, Emperor Gaozu, at Baideng in 200 BC."},{"name":"Yuezhi","kind":"appeared","why":"The Yuezhi were nomads in today's Gansu, in western China. The Xiongnu would soon drive them west, a migration that shaped Central Asia and India."},{"name":"Scythians","kind":"appeared","why":"The map uses Scythians as a broad label for the Iranian-speaking nomads of the steppe, including Saka and Sarmatians."},{"name":"Seleucid Kingdom","kind":"shrank","why":"The Seleucids lost Bactria, which became an independent Greek kingdom, and Parthia, which was taken by the Parni nomads, both around 250 to 240 BC."}],"spotlight":[{"name":"Carthage","fact":"Hannibal famously crossed the Alps with elephants, but Rome finally defeated him at Zama in 202 BC."},{"name":"Bactria","fact":"The Greco-Bactrian kingdom in Afghanistan was so rich it was called the land of a thousand cities; its kings later invaded India."},{"name":"Mauryan Empire","fact":"Ashoka's edicts, carved on rocks and pillars across India, are among the oldest deciphered writing in the subcontinent."}]},{"year":-100,"title":"The Silk Road opens","overview":"The Han emperor Wu pushed Chinese power into Central Asia, and the Silk Road linked China with Persia and the Mediterranean. Rome now dominated the Mediterranean but was torn by civil strife, and Parthia ruled Iran and Mesopotamia.","india":"The map still shows the Mauryan Empire, but the Mauryas had fallen around 185 BC; the Shunga dynasty ruled Magadha. Indo-Greek kings held the northwest, the Satavahanas were rising in the Deccan, and Kalinga flourished.","changes":[{"name":"Kushan Empire","kind":"appeared","why":"The Yuezhi had conquered Bactria around 130 BC. The map gives them the name of the Kushan Empire their descendants founded in the 1st century AD."},{"name":"Yuezhi","kind":"vanished","why":"The Yuezhi left their homeland in western China after defeats by the Xiongnu and moved to Bactria, shown here as the Kushans."},{"name":"Scythians","kind":"shrank","why":"The map now splits the steppe nomads into separate groups, such as the Sarmatians (Sarmates) and the Saka (Saces)."},{"name":"Eastern North American hunter-gatherers","kind":"vanished","why":"The map now shows eastern North America as named regional cultures, such as the Hopewell, famous for their earthwork mounds."}],"spotlight":[{"name":"Han Empire","fact":"Emperor Wu sent the envoy Zhang Qian to Central Asia; his reports helped open the trade routes later called the Silk Road."},{"name":"Nazca","fact":"The Nazca of southern Peru began drawing huge lines and animal figures in the desert, best seen from the air."},{"name":"Parthia","fact":"Parthian horse archers were famous for the Parthian shot, turning in the saddle to shoot backwards while retreating."}]},{"year":-1,"title":"Rome and Han at their height","overview":"Two great empires dominated Eurasia: Rome, now ruled by its first emperor Augustus, and Han China. Between them, Parthia controlled Iran and Mesopotamia. Trade along the Silk Road and the Indian Ocean was booming.","india":"India was divided: Indo-Scythian Saka rulers held the northwest, the Satavahanas ruled the Deccan, and the Tamil kingdoms of the Cheras, Cholas and Pandyas flourished in the south, trading pepper and gems with Rome.","changes":[{"name":"Roman Empire","kind":"appeared","why":"After winning the civil wars and annexing Egypt in 30 BC, Octavian became Augustus, Rome's first emperor, in 27 BC. The Republic became an empire."},{"name":"Han","kind":"appeared","why":"Han China grew under Emperor Wu (141 to 87 BC), who conquered northern Korea, northern Vietnam and the corridor into Central Asia. The map also renames it."},{"name":"Han Empire","kind":"renamed","why":"Same state, renamed in the map data."},{"name":"Parthian Empire","kind":"appeared","why":"Parthia, renamed on the map, had grown into an empire: Mithridates I took Mesopotamia from the Seleucids, and the Parthians crushed Rome at Carrhae in 53 BC."},{"name":"Seleucid Kingdom","kind":"vanished","why":"The shrunken Seleucid kingdom was annexed by the Roman general Pompey in 63 BC and became the Roman province of Syria."},{"name":"Mauryan Empire","kind":"vanished","why":"The Mauryan dynasty had ended around 185 BC, when the last emperor was killed by his general Pushyamitra Shunga; the map now removes it."},{"name":"Saka Kingdom","kind":"appeared","why":"Saka nomads, pushed south by the Yuezhi, entered northwest India; kings like Maues founded Indo-Scythian kingdoms in the 1st century BC."}],"spotlight":[{"name":"Judea","fact":"Herod the Great, a client king of Rome, rebuilt the Temple in Jerusalem; he died around 4 BC."},{"name":"Satavahanihara","fact":"The Satavahanas ruled the Deccan for about four centuries and supported Buddhist sites like the caves at Karla."},{"name":"Axum","fact":"Axum in Ethiopia was growing into a trading kingdom that later minted its own coins and adopted Christianity."}]},{"year":100,"title":"Trajan, Kushans and the Silk Road","overview":"Rome under Trajan was nearing its greatest extent, and Han China dominated East Asia. Across the middle, the Kushan Empire controlled key Silk Road routes. The Han had broken the northern Xiongnu, opening the steppe to new peoples.","india":"The Kushans ruled from Afghanistan into the Ganga plain, with Mathura and Gandhara famed for Buddhist art. The Satavahanas held the Deccan, and Tamil ports like Muziris and Arikamedu traded with Roman ships.","changes":[{"name":"Kushan Empire","kind":"appeared","why":"Kujula Kadphises united the Yuezhi tribes in the 1st century AD. His successors conquered the northwest of India and pushed into the Ganga plain."},{"name":"Xiongnu","kind":"vanished","why":"The Xiongnu split in two. Han armies crushed the northern Xiongnu in 89 AD, and the Xianbei took over the Mongolian steppe."},{"name":"S. Xiongnu","kind":"appeared","why":"The Southern Xiongnu submitted to the Han in 50 AD and were settled along China's northern frontier as allies."},{"name":"Suren Kingdom","kind":"vanished","why":"This Indo-Parthian kingdom, famous for King Gondophares, lost its lands in the northwest to the expanding Kushans."},{"name":"Alans","kind":"appeared","why":"The Alans, Iranian-speaking nomads related to the Sarmatians, dominated the steppe north of the Caucasus and raided into Parthia and Armenia."},{"name":"Yayoi","kind":"appeared","why":"The map now shows Japan's Yayoi culture, rice farmers who used bronze and iron and lived in growing chiefdoms."}],"spotlight":[{"name":"Teotihuacan","fact":"Teotihuacan in Mexico was becoming one of the world's biggest cities, built around the huge Pyramid of the Sun."},{"name":"Moche","fact":"The Moche of northern Peru made lifelike portrait pottery and built huge adobe pyramids."},{"name":"Paekche","fact":"Paekche, one of Korea's Three Kingdoms, later helped carry Buddhism and Chinese writing to Japan."}]},{"year":200,"title":"Rome, Parthia and a fading Han","overview":"Rome is at its height under the Severan emperors, Parthia still rules Iran, and Han China is breaking apart as warlords fight over a powerless court. Most of the world's land is still home to hunters, herders and village farmers outside any state.","india":"The Satavahanas still hold much of the Deccan, the Cholas and other Tamil kingdoms trade with Rome from the south, and the Kushan power in the north is fading. In the Ganges valley, Magadha is the old heartland from which the Guptas will soon rise.","changes":[{"name":"Kushan Empire","kind":"vanished","why":"After the great Kanishka, Kushan power weakened. By the early 200s it was losing ground in India, and soon Sasanian Persia would take its western lands."},{"name":"Hindu kingdoms","kind":"vanished","why":"The map stops lumping north India together and shows named realms such as Magadha instead."},{"name":"Magadha","kind":"appeared","why":"The old Ganges kingdom of the Mauryas reappears as local rulers fill the gap left by the Kushans. The Gupta dynasty will soon build an empire from here."},{"name":"Suren Kingdom","kind":"appeared","why":"The Indo-Parthian realm of the Suren family, a powerful Parthian noble clan, held Sistan and lands toward the Indus."},{"name":"Sámi","kind":"renamed","why":"Same state, renamed in the map data."},{"name":"Saami","kind":"renamed","why":"Same state, renamed in the map data."}],"spotlight":[{"name":"Teotihuacan","fact":"With perhaps 100,000 people, Teotihuacan in central Mexico was one of the largest cities on Earth, laid out around the vast Pyramid of the Sun."},{"name":"Axum","fact":"Axum in Ethiopia was a major Red Sea trading power and later one of the first states to adopt Christianity, under King Ezana in the 300s."},{"name":"Moche","fact":"The Moche of coastal Peru built huge adobe pyramids and made some of the most lifelike portrait pottery of the ancient world."}]},{"year":300,"title":"Rome split four ways, Han gone","overview":"Rome survived its third-century crisis by being divided among four rulers, the Tetrarchy set up by Diocletian. In China the Han fell in 220, and after the Three Kingdoms period the Jin dynasty briefly reunited the country in 280.","india":"In Magadha a new family, the Guptas, is gaining strength. Around 320 Chandragupta I will take the title of great king of kings, beginning the empire often called India's classical golden age.","changes":[{"name":"Roman Empire","kind":"vanished","why":"Not conquered but reorganized: from 293 Diocletian's Tetrarchy shared the empire among two senior and two junior emperors, each with his own territory."},{"name":"Rome (Diocletianus)","kind":"appeared","why":"Diocletian, senior emperor in the east, ruled from Nicomedia and governed the eastern provinces under the Tetrarchy."},{"name":"Rome (Maximian)","kind":"appeared","why":"Maximian, Diocletian's co-emperor, ruled Italy and Africa in the west under the Tetrarchy."},{"name":"Rome (Constantinus)","kind":"appeared","why":"Constantius Chlorus, junior emperor of the west and father of Constantine the Great, governed Gaul and Britain."},{"name":"Han","kind":"vanished","why":"The Han dynasty ended in 220, when the last emperor abdicated, and China split into the Three Kingdoms of Wei, Shu and Wu."},{"name":"Jin","kind":"appeared","why":"Sima Yan founded the Jin dynasty in 265 and conquered Wu in 280, briefly reuniting China."},{"name":"Gupta Empire","kind":"appeared","why":"The Gupta family rose in Magadha in the late 200s. Their capital region around Pataliputra became the core of a great empire."},{"name":"Magadha","kind":"vanished","why":"Magadha is now the heartland of the rising Gupta state."},{"name":"Saami","kind":"renamed","why":"Same state, renamed in the map data."},{"name":"Sámi","kind":"renamed","why":"Same state, renamed in the map data."}],"spotlight":[{"name":"Empire of Ghana","fact":"Ancient Ghana, in today's Mauritania and Mali, grew rich by taxing gold carried north across the Sahara. It lay far from modern Ghana."},{"name":"Koguryo","fact":"Koguryo, one of Korea's Three Kingdoms, ruled north Korea and Manchuria. The modern name Korea comes from its later successor, Goryeo."},{"name":"Simhala","fact":"Sri Lanka's kings at Anuradhapura built enormous reservoirs and stupas; the Jetavanaramaya was among the tallest buildings in the ancient world."}]},{"year":400,"title":"Huns on the move, Guptas at peak","overview":"Rome has split for good into western and eastern halves since 395, and the Huns have burst out of the steppe, pushing Goths and other peoples into Roman lands. Persia is ruled by the Sasanians, and the Rouran dominate Mongolia.","india":"The Gupta Empire is at its peak under Chandragupta II (Vikramaditya), who conquered the Saka rulers of Gujarat and Malwa. This is the age of Kalidasa's poetry, and the Chinese pilgrim Faxian is about to visit.","changes":[{"name":"Ruanruan","kind":"appeared","why":"The Rouran, a Mongolic people, built a steppe khaganate around 400 that ruled Mongolia until the Turks overthrew them in 552."},{"name":"Western Roman Empire","kind":"appeared","why":"After Theodosius I died in 395, the empire was divided for good between his sons. Honorius took the west."},{"name":"Eastern Roman Empire","kind":"appeared","why":"Arcadius, the other son of Theodosius I, took the east, ruled from Constantinople. This half would last another thousand years."},{"name":"Persi","kind":"appeared","why":"Persia under the Sasanian dynasty, which Ardashir I founded in 224 by overthrowing the Parthians."},{"name":"Parthian Empire","kind":"vanished","why":"The Parthian Arsacids were overthrown in 224 by Ardashir I, founder of the Sasanian dynasty."},{"name":"Gupta Empire","kind":"grew","why":"Samudragupta campaigned across India, and his son Chandragupta II conquered the Western Kshatrapas, adding Gujarat, Malwa and ports on the Arabian Sea."},{"name":"Rome (Diocletianus)","kind":"vanished","why":"The Tetrarchy broke down in civil wars, and Constantine the Great reunited the empire in 324."},{"name":"Rome (Maximian)","kind":"vanished","why":"The Tetrarchy broke down in civil wars, and Constantine the Great reunited the empire in 324."},{"name":"Hunnic Empire","kind":"appeared","why":"The Huns crossed the Volga around 370, crushed the Alans and the Gothic kingdom, and set off a wave of migrations into the Roman Empire."},{"name":"Saka Kingdom","kind":"vanished","why":"The Saka Western Kshatrapas of western India were defeated by the Gupta king Chandragupta II around the end of the 300s."}],"spotlight":[{"name":"Visigoths","fact":"Fleeing the Huns, the Visigoths entered Roman land in 376, beat an emperor at Adrianople in 378, and sacked Rome itself in 410."},{"name":"Tasmanian hunter-gatherers","fact":"Tasmanians had been cut off from mainland Australia by rising seas for about 10,000 years, one of the longest isolations of any human group."}]},{"year":500,"title":"After the fall of the West","overview":"The Western Roman Empire is gone: in 476 its last emperor was deposed, and Germanic kingdoms of Franks, Visigoths, Ostrogoths and Vandals rule its lands. The Eastern Roman Empire and Sasanian Persia remain the great powers, and north China is ruled by the Northern Wei.","india":"The Gupta Empire is weakening under attacks by the Alchon Huns, whose king Toramana pushed into north India around 500. In the Deccan and south, the Vakatakas, Kadambas, Pallavas and Pandyas hold their own kingdoms.","changes":[{"name":"Sasanian Empire","kind":"renamed","why":"Same state, renamed in the map data."},{"name":"Persi","kind":"renamed","why":"Same state, renamed in the map data."},{"name":"Jin","kind":"renamed","why":"Same state, renamed in the map data."},{"name":"Jin Empire","kind":"renamed","why":"Same state, renamed in the map data."},{"name":"Western Roman Empire","kind":"shrank","why":"Germanic kingdoms took province after province, and in 476 Odoacer deposed the boy emperor Romulus Augustulus. Only a small remnant is still drawn."},{"name":"Toba Wei","kind":"appeared","why":"The Northern Wei dynasty of the Tuoba clan, a Xianbei people, unified north China in 439 and adopted Chinese ways and Buddhism."},{"name":"Sixteen Kingdoms","kind":"vanished","why":"The many short-lived states of north China were swept up when the Northern Wei unified the north in 439."},{"name":"Hunnic Empire","kind":"vanished","why":"Attila died in 453, and the next year his subject peoples beat his sons at the Battle of Nedao. The Hunnic empire fell apart quickly."}],"spotlight":[{"name":"Franks","fact":"Clovis, king of the Franks, united most of Gaul and was baptized around 500. France takes its name from his people."},{"name":"Funan","fact":"Funan in the Mekong delta was an early Southeast Asian trading state; Roman coins have been found at its port of Oc Eo."},{"name":"Yamato","fact":"The Yamato kings of Japan were buried in giant keyhole-shaped mounds; the largest, linked to Emperor Nintoku, is longer than the Great Pyramid's base."}]},{"year":600,"title":"Sui China and the Turkic steppe","overview":"China is united again under the Sui dynasty, and the Göktürks have built a vast steppe empire stretching from Manchuria toward the Black Sea. The Eastern Roman Empire and Sasanian Persia are about to fight their last great war.","india":"The Gupta Empire is gone and India is ruled by regional kingdoms. The Chalukyas of Badami rise in the Deccan, the Pallavas rule from Kanchipuram, and in the north Harsha will soon build an empire from Kannauj, starting in 606.","changes":[{"name":"Sui Empire","kind":"appeared","why":"Yang Jian founded the Sui in 581 and conquered the southern Chen dynasty in 589, reuniting China after nearly 300 years of division."},{"name":"Göktürks","kind":"appeared","why":"In 552 the Turks under Bumin rebelled against their Rouran overlords and built the first Turkic khaganate across the steppe."},{"name":"Ruanruan","kind":"vanished","why":"The Rouran Khaganate was destroyed by its former vassals, the Göktürks, in 552 and the years after."},{"name":"Jin Empire","kind":"vanished","why":"The southern Chinese dynasties ended when the Sui conquered the Chen in 589."},{"name":"Toba Wei","kind":"vanished","why":"The Northern Wei split into Eastern and Western Wei in 534; their successors were absorbed by the Sui."},{"name":"Gupta Empire","kind":"vanished","why":"Hun invasions, lost trade and rebellious governors broke the Gupta Empire apart by the mid-500s."},{"name":"Hindu kingdoms","kind":"appeared","why":"After the Guptas, north India split among regional dynasties such as the Maukharis, Later Guptas and Pushyabhutis."},{"name":"Axum","kind":"shrank","why":"Axum lost its hold on Yemen when Persia took it around 570, and its Red Sea trade declined."},{"name":"Hopewell Culture","kind":"vanished","why":"The Hopewell trade network of eastern North America, famous for its earthworks, faded around 500 for reasons still debated."}],"spotlight":[{"name":"Kamarupa","fact":"Kamarupa in Assam was ruled by the Varman dynasty; its king Bhaskaravarman was an ally of Harsha and hosted the pilgrim Xuanzang."},{"name":"Huari Empire","fact":"The Wari of highland Peru built an early Andean empire with planned administrative cities and roads, centuries before the Inca."},{"name":"Tiahuanaco Empire","fact":"Tiwanaku, near Lake Titicaca, grew food at 3,800 metres using raised fields and was the religious center of the southern Andes."}]},{"year":700,"title":"The Arab conquests","overview":"In less than a century the Arabs, united by Islam, have conquered Persia, Syria, Egypt and North Africa. The Umayyad Caliphate rules from Damascus, Tibet has become an empire, and Byzantium has lost its richest provinces.","india":"North India is a patchwork of kingdoms after Harsha's death in 647. The Chalukyas and Pallavas fight over the Deccan and south, and in 712 the Arab general Muhammad bin Qasim will conquer Sind from Raja Dahir.","changes":[{"name":"Tufan Empire","kind":"appeared","why":"Songtsen Gampo unified Tibet in the early 600s, making it a major Asian power that would rival Tang China for two centuries."},{"name":"Umayyad Caliphate","kind":"appeared","why":"After the death of Muhammad in 632, Arab armies conquered Syria, Egypt and Persia. Muawiya founded the Umayyad Caliphate in 661, ruling from Damascus."},{"name":"Western Gokturk Khaganate","kind":"appeared","why":"The Turkic khaganate split around 600 into eastern and western halves; the western one ruled Central Asia's Silk Road cities."},{"name":"Eastern Roman Empire","kind":"shrank","why":"Defeat at Yarmouk in 636 lost Syria, Egypt followed in 641, and Carthage fell in 698. Byzantium kept Anatolia, the Balkan coasts and parts of Italy."},{"name":"Hindu kingdoms","kind":"shrank","why":"The map now shows more named kingdoms such as Kashmir, Sind, Kanauj and the Chalukyas instead of one broad label."}],"spotlight":[{"name":"Kingdom of Sind","fact":"Sind's Raja Dahir was the last Hindu king of the region; its conquest in 712 brought Islam to South Asia by land."},{"name":"Khazars","fact":"The Khazar Khaganate north of the Caucasus blocked Arab advances north, and its ruling class later adopted Judaism."},{"name":"Silla","fact":"Silla, allied with Tang China, conquered its rivals Paekche and Koguryo in the 660s and unified most of Korea."}]},{"year":800,"title":"Baghdad, Chang'an and Aachen","overview":"Three great powers dominate: the Abbasid Caliphate under Harun al-Rashid, Tang China, and the Tibetan Empire. In Europe, Charlemagne is crowned emperor in Rome on Christmas Day 800. The map also now shows the Bantu-speaking farmers of central and southern Africa.","india":"The Palas of Bengal, the Gurjara-Pratiharas of Rajasthan and the Rashtrakutas of the Deccan are locked in the 'tripartite struggle' for control of Kannauj, the prize city of north India.","changes":[{"name":"Bantu peoples","kind":"appeared","why":"Over the previous two thousand years Bantu-speaking farmers spread iron working and farming across central, eastern and southern Africa. The map now labels them."},{"name":"Khoisan","kind":"shrank","why":"Khoisan herders and foragers held on in the south and west as Bantu-speaking farmers settled much of southern Africa."},{"name":"Abbasid Caliphate","kind":"appeared","why":"The Abbasids overthrew the Umayyads in 750, moved the capital east, and founded Baghdad in 762."},{"name":"Tibetan Empire","kind":"renamed","why":"Same state, renamed in the map data."},{"name":"Sui Empire","kind":"vanished","why":"The Sui dynasty collapsed after costly wars in Korea; Li Yuan founded the Tang in 618."},{"name":"Tang Empire","kind":"appeared","why":"The Tang, founded in 618, made Chang'an the world's largest city, though the An Lushan rebellion of 755 weakened it."},{"name":"Göktürks","kind":"vanished","why":"The last Turkic khaganate in Mongolia was overthrown in 744 by its subjects, the Uyghurs."},{"name":"Uyghurs","kind":"grew","why":"The Uyghurs founded their own khaganate in 744 and helped the Tang crush the An Lushan rebellion."}],"spotlight":[{"name":"Carolingian Empire","fact":"Charlemagne was crowned emperor by Pope Leo III on Christmas Day 800, reviving the title in western Europe."},{"name":"Srivijaya Empire","fact":"Srivijaya, based in Sumatra, controlled the Malacca Strait and was a great center of Buddhist learning, visited by pilgrims on their way to India."},{"name":"Rashtrakuta state","fact":"The Rashtrakutas carved the Kailasa temple at Ellora from a single cliff of rock, starting from the top down."}]},{"year":900,"title":"The caliphate breaks apart","overview":"The Abbasid caliph in Baghdad still has prestige but has lost real power over much of his empire, as governors and rebels found their own dynasties. Tang China is in its last years, and Vikings, Magyars and Arabs raid Europe.","india":"The Pratiharas rule a large north Indian empire from Kannauj, facing the Palas and Rashtrakutas. In the far south, Aditya I of the Cholas has just overthrown the Pallavas, starting the rise of the Chola Empire.","changes":[{"name":"Abbasid Caliphate","kind":"shrank","why":"After the turmoil at Samarra in the 860s, provinces broke away: the Saffarids and Samanids in the east, the Tulunids in Egypt, the Aghlabids in Tunisia."},{"name":"Saffarids","kind":"appeared","why":"Yaqub ibn al-Layth, a coppersmith turned soldier from Sistan, founded the Saffarid state around 861 and even marched on Baghdad."},{"name":"Samanid Empire","kind":"appeared","why":"The Persian Samanids ruled Central Asia and eastern Iran from Bukhara. Under Ismail Samani they revived Persian literature."},{"name":"Slavonic tribes","kind":"renamed","why":"Same state, renamed in the map data."}],"spotlight":[{"name":"Khmer Empire","fact":"Jayavarman II founded the Khmer state in 802; around 900 Yasovarman I built a new capital at Angkor, later home to Angkor Wat."},{"name":"Kyivan Rus","fact":"Around 882 the Varangian prince Oleg took Kyiv and made it the center of a state linking the Baltic to the Black Sea."},{"name":"Cholas","fact":"The Cholas' rise began when Vijayalaya took Thanjavur around 850; within 150 years they would rule much of south India and beyond."}]},{"year":1000,"title":"Song China and the Chola navy","overview":"China has been reunited by the Song dynasty, a wealthy age of printing, paper money and big cities. Baghdad's caliphs are controlled by the Persian Buyids, the Fatimids rule Egypt, and Europe sees new Christian kingdoms in Poland, Hungary and Kyivan Rus.","india":"Rajaraja I (985 to 1014) has made the Chola Empire the strongest power in the south, taking northern Sri Lanka. In the northwest, Mahmud of Ghazni begins his famous raids into India with his defeat of King Jayapala in 1001.","changes":[{"name":"Abbasid Caliphate","kind":"vanished","why":"In 945 the Buyids, a Persian dynasty, took Baghdad. The Abbasid caliphs lived on as religious figureheads under their control."},{"name":"Tibetan Empire","kind":"vanished","why":"The Tibetan Empire broke apart after King Langdarma was assassinated around 842, and Tibet split into local lordships."},{"name":"Tang Empire","kind":"vanished","why":"Rebellions and warlords destroyed the Tang, which ended in 907. China then split into the Five Dynasties and Ten Kingdoms."},{"name":"Song Empire","kind":"appeared","why":"General Zhao Kuangyin seized power in 960 and founded the Song, which reunited most of China by 979."},{"name":"Kimek-Kipchak khaganate","kind":"appeared","why":"Kimek and Kipchak Turkic tribes formed a loose confederation across the steppe of today's Kazakhstan."}],"spotlight":[{"name":"Chola Empire","fact":"Rajaraja I's Brihadeeswarar temple in Thanjavur, completed in 1010, has a granite tower about 66 metres tall."},{"name":"Icelandic Commonwealth","fact":"Iceland had no king; its chieftains met at the Althing, founded in 930, one of the oldest parliaments in the world."},{"name":"Liao","fact":"The Khitan Liao dynasty ruled north of China; the word 'Cathay', and the Russian word for China, come from the Khitan name."}]},{"year":1100,"title":"Seljuks, Crusaders and Cholas","overview":"The Seljuk Turks have conquered much of the Middle East, and their victory at Manzikert in 1071 helped spark the First Crusade, which took Jerusalem in 1099. In north Africa and Spain the Almoravids rule, and new Turkic peoples fill the steppe.","india":"The Chola Empire under Kulottunga I still dominates the south and east coast, while the Western Chalukyas rule the Deccan. North India is divided among Rajput dynasties such as the Chauhans, Chandellas and Paramaras, and the Ghaznavids hold Punjab.","changes":[{"name":"Cuman-Kipchak confederation","kind":"appeared","why":"The Cumans and Kipchaks pushed west across the steppe in the 1000s, reaching the borders of Rus, Byzantium and Hungary."},{"name":"Kimek-Kipchak khaganate","kind":"vanished","why":"The Kimek confederation broke up and its Kipchak members moved west, forming the Cuman-Kipchak confederation."},{"name":"Mongols","kind":"appeared","why":"Mongol-speaking clans lived as herders on the Mongolian steppe, still divided and not yet united."},{"name":"Denmark-Norway","kind":"vanished","why":"Cnut the Great's North Sea empire fell apart after his death in 1035, and Denmark and Norway went separate ways."},{"name":"Almoravid dynasty","kind":"appeared","why":"Berber reformers from the Sahara founded Marrakesh around 1070. Under Yusuf ibn Tashfin they conquered Morocco and Muslim Spain, beating Castile in 1086."},{"name":"Buwayhid Emirates","kind":"vanished","why":"The Seljuk leader Tughril Beg entered Baghdad in 1055 and ended Buyid rule."},{"name":"Seljuk Empire","kind":"appeared","why":"Seljuk Turks took Iran and Iraq, beat Byzantium at Manzikert in 1071, and peaked under Sultan Malik Shah."},{"name":"Karkhanids","kind":"appeared","why":"The Kara-Khanids, a Turkic Muslim dynasty, took Bukhara from the Samanids in 999 and ruled Central Asia's cities."},{"name":"Kievan Rus","kind":"renamed","why":"Same state, renamed in the map data."},{"name":"Rajput kingdoms","kind":"vanished","why":"The map now shows north India's Rajput dynasties by name, such as the Chandellas, Chauhans, Solankis and Paramaras."}],"spotlight":[{"name":"Chandellas","fact":"The Chandellas built the temples of Khajuraho, famous for their detailed carvings, mostly between about 950 and 1050."},{"name":"Đại Việt","fact":"Đại Việt won independence from China in 938; in 1076 its general Lý Thường Kiệt defeated a Song invasion."},{"name":"Great Zimbabwe","fact":"Great Zimbabwe's stone walls, built without mortar, enclosed the capital of a kingdom that grew rich on gold traded to the Indian Ocean."}]},{"year":1200,"title":"Genghis Khan rises","overview":"On the Mongolian steppe, Temujin is uniting the tribes and will be proclaimed Genghis Khan in 1206. Saladin has retaken Jerusalem in 1187, the Almohads rule Morocco and Spain, and China is divided between the Song and the Jurchen Jin.","india":"The Ghurid conqueror Muhammad of Ghor defeated Prithviraj Chauhan at the Second Battle of Tarain in 1192. His general Qutb ud-Din Aibak holds Delhi and will found the Delhi Sultanate in 1206.","changes":[{"name":"Mongol Empire","kind":"appeared","why":"Temujin defeated rival tribes one by one and in 1206 was proclaimed Genghis Khan, ruler of all the Mongols."},{"name":"Mongols","kind":"vanished","why":"The scattered Mongol clans are now drawn as the unified Mongol Empire."},{"name":"Kara Khitai Khaganate","kind":"appeared","why":"When the Jurchen destroyed the Liao in 1125, the Khitan prince Yelu Dashi fled west and founded the Kara Khitai, or Western Liao, in Central Asia."},{"name":"Cuman Khanates","kind":"appeared","why":"The Cuman-Kipchak tribes, now drawn as separate khanates, still dominate the western steppe."},{"name":"Cuman-Kipchak confederation","kind":"vanished","why":"The map now shows the same Cuman-Kipchak tribes as separate khanates."},{"name":"Almoravid dynasty","kind":"vanished","why":"The Almohads, a rival Berber reform movement, took Marrakesh in 1147 and replaced the Almoravids."},{"name":"Seljuk Empire","kind":"vanished","why":"The Great Seljuk state broke up after Sultan Sanjar died in 1157, and the Khwarazmians took its eastern lands."}],"spotlight":[{"name":"Sultanate of Delhi","fact":"Qutb ud-Din Aibak began the Qutb Minar in Delhi in the 1190s; finished by his successor Iltutmish, it is about 73 metres tall."},{"name":"Khmer Empire","fact":"Under Jayavarman VII the Khmer built Angkor Thom and the Bayon temple, with its many giant smiling stone faces."},{"name":"Angevin Empire","fact":"Henry II and his sons ruled England and about half of France; Richard the Lionheart spent only months of his reign in England."}]},{"year":1279,"title":"The largest land empire","overview":"The Mongols have built the largest connected land empire in history, from Korea to Hungary's borders. In 1279 Kublai Khan finished conquering Song China. In practice the empire is splitting into four parts: Yuan China, the Golden Horde, the Ilkhanate and the Chagatai lands.","india":"Sultan Balban rules the Delhi Sultanate with an iron hand, guarding Punjab against Mongol raids. In the south, the Pandyas of Madurai are now the strongest Tamil power, having overtaken the declining Cholas.","changes":[{"name":"Mongol Empire","kind":"grew","why":"Genghis Khan and his heirs conquered Khwarazm (1219 to 1221), Rus (1237 to 1240), Baghdad (1258) and finally Song China, ending with the naval battle of Yamen in 1279."},{"name":"Kara Khitai Khaganate","kind":"vanished","why":"The Mongol general Jebe conquered Kara Khitai in 1218."},{"name":"Song Empire","kind":"vanished","why":"Kublai Khan's forces took Hangzhou in 1276, and the last Song loyalists were destroyed at the Battle of Yamen in 1279."},{"name":"Tuareg Nomadic Tribes","kind":"renamed","why":"Same state, renamed in the map data."},{"name":"Touareg","kind":"renamed","why":"Same state, renamed in the map data."}],"spotlight":[{"name":"Mamluke Sultanate","fact":"Egypt's slave-soldier Mamluks were the first to stop the Mongols in a major battle, at Ain Jalut in Palestine in 1260."},{"name":"Mali","fact":"Sundiata Keita founded the Mali Empire around 1235; its wealth in gold made later king Mansa Musa famous across the world."},{"name":"Shogun Japan (Kamakura)","fact":"The Kamakura shogunate fought off Mongol invasions in 1274 and 1281, helped by storms the Japanese called kamikaze, 'divine wind'."}]},{"year":1300,"title":"Khanates and the Delhi Sultanate","overview":"The Mongol world is now a family of rival khanates: Yuan China, the Ilkhanate in Persia, the Golden Horde on the Russian steppe and the Chagatai Khanate in Central Asia. Trade along the Silk Road flourishes, and Marco Polo has just returned from China.","india":"Alauddin Khalji (1296 to 1316) is the most powerful Delhi sultan yet. He beat back Mongol invasions, took Ranthambore and Chittor, and his general Malik Kafur will soon raid deep into the south as far as Madurai.","changes":[{"name":"Mongol Empire","kind":"shrank","why":"After Möngke Khan died in 1259 the empire split in civil war. The map now draws the Ilkhanate and Golden Horde separately; Kublai's Yuan keeps the 'Mongol Empire' label."},{"name":"Ilkhanate","kind":"appeared","why":"Hulagu, Kublai's brother, founded the Ilkhanate in Persia after sacking Baghdad in 1258. Ghazan Khan converted to Islam in 1295."},{"name":"Khanate of the Golden Horde","kind":"appeared","why":"Founded by Batu, grandson of Genghis Khan, after the conquest of Rus; it ruled the western steppe and took tribute from Russian princes."},{"name":"Tibet","kind":"appeared","why":"Tibet was governed by the lamas of the Sakya school under the patronage of Yuan China."},{"name":"Novgorod","kind":"appeared","why":"The merchant republic of Novgorod controlled the fur trade of the far north and traded with the Hanseatic League."},{"name":"Samis","kind":"renamed","why":"Same state, renamed in the map data."},{"name":"Sámi","kind":"renamed","why":"Same state, renamed in the map data."}],"spotlight":[{"name":"Grand Duchy of Moscow","fact":"In 1300 Moscow was a minor principality under the Golden Horde; within 200 years it would unite Russia."},{"name":"Chimú Empire","fact":"Chan Chan, the Chimú capital on the Peru coast, was the largest adobe city in the Americas."},{"name":"Kashmir and Ladakh","fact":"Kashmir's last Hindu kings would give way in 1339 to the Shah Mir dynasty, the valley's first Muslim rulers."}]},{"year":1400,"title":"Timur and the Black Death's wake","overview":"Europe and Asia are recovering from the Black Death of the 1340s. Timur has carved an empire from Central Asia to Persia, sacking Delhi in 1398 and about to crush the Ottomans at Ankara in 1402. In China, the Ming replaced the Mongols in 1368.","india":"Timur's sack of Delhi in 1398 has left the Tughluq sultanate shattered. In the south, the Hindu Vijayanagara Empire (founded 1336) and the Bahmani Sultanate (founded 1347) are the rising powers, fighting over the Raichur doab.","changes":[{"name":"Timurid Empire","kind":"appeared","why":"Timur (Tamerlane), a Turkic-Mongol warlord from Samarkand, conquered Persia, Iraq and much of Central Asia from the 1370s."},{"name":"Ilkhanate","kind":"vanished","why":"The Ilkhanate broke apart after Abu Said died without an heir in 1335; Timur later conquered most of its lands."},{"name":"Kalmar Union","kind":"appeared","why":"In 1397 Queen Margaret I united Denmark, Norway and Sweden under one crown at Kalmar."},{"name":"Khanate of the Golden Horde","kind":"vanished","why":"Timur's wars against Khan Tokhtamysh in the 1390s shattered the Golden Horde, which the map now draws as the Blue and White Hordes."},{"name":"Sultanate of Delhi","kind":"grew","why":"The Tughluqs pushed the sultanate deep into the Deccan in the 1330s. It still spans much of the north here, but Timur's sack of Delhi in 1398 left it badly weakened."},{"name":"Poland-Lithuania","kind":"appeared","why":"In 1385 Grand Duke Jogaila of Lithuania married Queen Jadwiga of Poland, uniting the two states; in 1410 they beat the Teutonic Knights at Grunwald."}],"spotlight":[{"name":"Ottoman Empire","fact":"The Ottomans spread fast across Anatolia and the Balkans, but Sultan Bayezid I was captured by Timur at Ankara in 1402."},{"name":"Ayutthaya","fact":"Founded in 1351, Ayutthaya became the great Thai kingdom and one of Asia's busiest trading cities."},{"name":"Chūzan","fact":"Chūzan was one of three kingdoms on Okinawa; it united them in 1429 as the Ryukyu Kingdom, a trading hub between China and Japan."}]},{"year":1492,"title":"On the eve of the ocean age","overview":"In 1492 Spain takes Granada and Columbus reaches the Caribbean. The Ming rule China, the Ottomans have held Constantinople since 1453, and the Americas are home to the Aztec and Inca empires and hundreds of other nations, which this map now draws by name.","india":"The Lodi sultans rule Delhi, Gujarat and Bengal are rich sultanates, and the Bahmani state is splitting into the Deccan sultanates. Vijayanagara rules the south. In 1498 Vasco da Gama will reach Calicut by sea.","changes":[{"name":"Mongol Empire","kind":"vanished","why":"In 1368 the rebel leader Zhu Yuanzhang drove the Mongol Yuan dynasty out of China and founded the Ming as the Hongwu Emperor."},{"name":"Ming Empire","kind":"appeared","why":"The Ming, founded in 1368, rebuilt the Great Wall, moved the capital to Beijing in 1421 and sent Zheng He's treasure fleets to Africa."},{"name":"Chagatai Khanate","kind":"appeared","why":"The eastern Chagatai Khanate, also called Moghulistan, survived in the Tian Shan and Tarim basin as other Mongol states fell."},{"name":"Timurid Empire","kind":"vanished","why":"After Shah Rukh died in 1447, Timur's heirs fought each other and the empire broke into smaller Timurid emirates."},{"name":"Kalmar Union","kind":"vanished","why":"Sweden kept breaking away from Danish kings; after the Battle of Brunkeberg in 1471 it was effectively independent, and the map shows Denmark-Norway and Sweden."},{"name":"Eastern North American hunter-gatherers","kind":"vanished","why":"The map now draws the individual Indigenous nations of North America instead of one broad label."},{"name":"Dënéndeh","kind":"appeared","why":"The map now names the homeland of the Dene peoples of northwest Canada, who had lived there for thousands of years."},{"name":"Očhéthi Šakówiŋ","kind":"appeared","why":"The map now names the Seven Council Fires, the Dakota, Nakota and Lakota peoples often called the Sioux."},{"name":"Amazon hunter-gatherers","kind":"vanished","why":"The map now draws the individual peoples of the Amazon instead of one broad label."},{"name":"Savanna hunter-gatherers","kind":"vanished","why":"The map now shows individual peoples instead of one broad label."}],"spotlight":[{"name":"Vijayanagara","fact":"Its capital at Hampi was one of the world's largest and richest cities; visiting Persian and Portuguese travelers were amazed by its markets."},{"name":"Mexihcah (Triple Alliance)","fact":"The Aztec capital Tenochtitlan, built on an island in Lake Texcoco, had perhaps 200,000 people, more than most European cities."},{"name":"Songhai","fact":"Sunni Ali, who made Songhai West Africa's largest empire, died in 1492; Askia Muhammad took power the next year."}]},{"year":1500,"title":"Dawn of the ocean age","overview":"Around 1500 the great land empires of Asia still dominate the map, from Ming China to the khanates of the steppe. But Europe is turning outward: Columbus reached the Caribbean in 1492 and Vasco da Gama reached India in 1498, opening sea routes that will reshape the world.","india":"The Lodi dynasty rules the Delhi Sultanate in the north, while the Hindu empire of Vijayanagara dominates the south. The old Bahmani Sultanate of the Deccan is splitting into rival sultanates such as Bijapur, Ahmadnagar and Bidar.","changes":[{"name":"Ming Chinese Empire","kind":"renamed","why":"Same state, renamed in the map data."},{"name":"Ming Empire","kind":"renamed","why":"Same state, renamed in the map data."},{"name":"Kalmar Union","kind":"appeared","why":"The Kalmar Union joined Denmark, Norway and Sweden under one crown from 1397. It is drawn here as a single bloc, though Sweden was often in revolt against Danish kings."}],"spotlight":[{"name":"Vijayanagara","fact":"Its capital Hampi was one of the largest cities in the world around 1500, and visiting Portuguese and Persian travellers described its markets of gems and horses with amazement."},{"name":"Inca Empire","fact":"The Incas ran an empire of millions along the Andes without wheeled vehicles or a written script, keeping records on knotted cords called quipu."},{"name":"Aztec Empire","fact":"Its capital Tenochtitlan was built on an island in Lake Texcoco and was larger than most European cities of the time."}]},{"year":1530,"title":"Age of gunpowder empires","overview":"Gunpowder empires are on the rise. The Ottomans have swallowed Egypt and much of Hungary, the Safavids rule Persia, and Babur has founded the Mughal Empire in India. Across the Atlantic, Spain has toppled the Aztecs and set up New Spain.","india":"In 1526 Babur, a Timurid prince, defeated Ibrahim Lodi at the First Battle of Panipat and founded the Mughal Empire; he died in 1530 and Humayun took the throne. In the south, Vijayanagara had just had its golden age under Krishnadevaraya.","changes":[{"name":"Ottoman Empire","kind":"grew","why":"Selim I conquered the Mamluk lands of Syria and Egypt in 1516-17, and Suleiman the Magnificent took Belgrade (1521), Rhodes (1522) and crushed Hungary at Mohacs in 1526."},{"name":"Kalmar Union","kind":"vanished","why":"Sweden broke away from the union in 1523 when Gustav Vasa was elected king, leaving Denmark-Norway on its own."},{"name":"Grand Duchy of Moscow","kind":"renamed","why":"Same state, renamed in the map data."},{"name":"Quazaq Khanate","kind":"appeared","why":"The Kazakh Khanate, founded by Kerei and Janibek around the 1460s, grew strong under Kasym Khan in the early 1500s and came to rule much of the steppe north of Central Asia."}],"spotlight":[{"name":"Mughal Empire","fact":"Babur's army at Panipat used field guns and matchlocks, among the first times gunpowder artillery decided a major battle in India."},{"name":"Safavid Empire","fact":"Founded by Shah Ismail I in 1501, it made Shia Islam the state religion of Iran, shaping the country's identity to this day."},{"name":"Viceroyalty of New Spain","fact":"Built on the ruins of the Aztec capital, which Hernan Cortes and his Indigenous allies captured in 1521, it became the heart of Spain's American empire."}]},{"year":1600,"title":"Tsars, shahs and Akbar","overview":"By 1600 Muscovy has pushed east across the Volga and into Siberia, the Ottomans and Safavids face each other across the Middle East, and Europe's trading companies are just forming. The English East India Company receives its charter on 31 December 1600.","india":"Akbar (r. 1556-1605) has expanded the Mughal Empire across the north, taking Gujarat, Bengal and Kashmir, and in 1600 his forces captured Ahmadnagar fort. Vijayanagara never recovered from its defeat at Talikota in 1565, when Hampi was sacked.","changes":[{"name":"Tsardom of Muscovy","kind":"grew","why":"Ivan IV (the Terrible) conquered Kazan in 1552 and Astrakhan in 1556, opening the Volga. From 1582 the Cossack Yermak led Russia's push across the Urals into Siberia."},{"name":"Kazan Khanate","kind":"vanished","why":"Ivan the Terrible captured Kazan in 1552 after a long siege, an event celebrated by building St Basil's Cathedral in Moscow."},{"name":"Khanate of Sibir","kind":"vanished","why":"Cossacks under Yermak took its capital in 1582, and Khan Kuchum's last resistance was broken in 1598, opening Siberia to Russian expansion."},{"name":"Nogai Horde","kind":"shrank","why":"Squeezed by Muscovy after the fall of Kazan and Astrakhan, the Nogais split apart, with some groups moving west towards the Crimean Khanate."}],"spotlight":[{"name":"Mughal Empire","fact":"Akbar built a new capital at Fatehpur Sikri and hosted debates between Hindu, Muslim, Jain, Zoroastrian and Christian scholars."},{"name":"Safavid Empire","fact":"Shah Abbas I made Isfahan his capital in 1598 and filled it with grand squares, mosques and bridges still admired today."},{"name":"Japan (Warring States)","fact":"In 1600 Tokugawa Ieyasu won the Battle of Sekigahara, ending over a century of civil war and setting up the shogunate that ruled Japan until 1868."}]},{"year":1650,"title":"Ming falls, the Qing rise","overview":"The mid-1600s were a time of crisis. The Ming dynasty collapsed in 1644 and the Manchus took Beijing, Europe emerged from the Thirty Years' War with the Peace of Westphalia in 1648, and Russian explorers reached the Pacific.","india":"Shah Jahan (r. 1628-1658) rules the Mughal Empire at its most magnificent: the Taj Mahal is nearing completion and the Red Fort in his new city of Shahjahanabad (Delhi) opened in 1648. In the Deccan, a young Shivaji has begun capturing hill forts from Bijapur.","changes":[{"name":"Ming Chinese Empire","kind":"vanished","why":"Rebels under Li Zicheng took Beijing in 1644 and the last Ming emperor took his own life. Ming loyalists fought on in the south for years."},{"name":"Manchu Empire","kind":"appeared","why":"The Manchus' Qing dynasty entered Beijing in 1644, invited through the Great Wall by Ming general Wu Sangui, and began conquering the rest of China."},{"name":"Tsardom of Muscovy","kind":"grew","why":"Russian fur traders and Cossacks raced across Siberia, founding Yakutsk in 1632 and reaching the Pacific coast in 1639."},{"name":"Spanish Habsburg","kind":"appeared","why":"The map now draws Spain and its vast American viceroyalties together as the Habsburg Spanish monarchy, ruled from Madrid by Philip IV."},{"name":"Viceroyalty of New Spain","kind":"vanished","why":"Now drawn as part of Spanish Habsburg rather than as a separate shape."},{"name":"Netherlands","kind":"appeared","why":"After an 80-year revolt against Spain, the Dutch Republic's independence was confirmed at the Peace of Munster in 1648. It was now Europe's leading trading power."},{"name":"Islamic and Hindu states","kind":"shrank","why":"The Mughals pushed into the Deccan, annexing Ahmadnagar in 1636 and forcing Bijapur and Golkonda to accept Mughal overlordship."},{"name":"Morocco","kind":"appeared","why":"Morocco was ruled by the Saadi dynasty, which had crushed Portugal at the Battle of the Three Kings in 1578 and sent an army across the Sahara to conquer Songhai in 1591."}],"spotlight":[{"name":"Tokugawa shogunate","fact":"In the 1630s it banned most foreign contact, allowing only Dutch and Chinese traders at Nagasaki, a policy later called sakoku."},{"name":"Kandy","fact":"This mountain kingdom in Sri Lanka allied with the Dutch against the Portuguese and stayed independent until the British took it in 1815."},{"name":"Mughal Empire","fact":"Shah Jahan's Peacock Throne was so encrusted with gems that it reportedly cost twice as much as the Taj Mahal."}]},{"year":1700,"title":"Aurangzeb at the summit","overview":"Around 1700 the Mughal, Qing and Ottoman empires still loom large, but Europe is changing: the Habsburgs pushed the Ottomans out of Hungary after the siege of Vienna in 1683, and Peter the Great is modernising Russia.","india":"Aurangzeb (r. 1658-1707) has taken the Mughal Empire to its largest size by conquering Bijapur (1686) and Golkonda (1687). But he is bogged down in a long war against the Marathas, whose king Shivaji was crowned in 1674.","changes":[{"name":"Athabaskan","kind":"renamed","why":"Same state, renamed in the map data."},{"name":"Athabascan","kind":"renamed","why":"Same state, renamed in the map data."},{"name":"Mughal Empire","kind":"grew","why":"Aurangzeb personally led campaigns into the Deccan, annexing the sultanates of Bijapur in 1686 and Golkonda in 1687."},{"name":"Islamic and Hindu states","kind":"vanished","why":"The remaining Deccan sultanates were absorbed by Aurangzeb's Mughal Empire in the 1680s."},{"name":"Golkonda","kind":"vanished","why":"Aurangzeb captured the fortress of Golkonda in 1687 after an eight-month siege, ending the Qutb Shahi dynasty, famed for its diamond trade."},{"name":"Vijayanagara","kind":"vanished","why":"After Talikota (1565) the empire survived as a shrinking rump under the Aravidu dynasty, which lost its last lands by the mid-1600s."},{"name":"Hindu kingdoms","kind":"appeared","why":"Smaller Hindu realms filled the gap in the far south, such as the Nayakas of Madurai and Thanjavur and the Wodeyars of Mysore."},{"name":"Austrian Empire","kind":"grew","why":"After the Ottoman siege of Vienna failed in 1683, the Habsburgs fought back and won Hungary and Transylvania at the Treaty of Karlowitz in 1699."}],"spotlight":[{"name":"Tibet","fact":"The 5th Dalai Lama built the Potala Palace in Lhasa from 1645, which became the seat of Tibet's government."},{"name":"Kandy","fact":"Its Temple of the Tooth guards a relic of the Buddha, and possessing it was seen as a sign of the right to rule Sri Lanka."},{"name":"Safavid Empire","fact":"Its capital Isfahan was so splendid that Persians said 'Isfahan is half the world'."}]},{"year":1715,"title":"New crowns after the wars","overview":"Europe has just ended the War of the Spanish Succession (Treaties of Utrecht, 1713-14), which put a Bourbon king on Spain's throne. England and Scotland have united as Great Britain, and Qing China has expanded under the Kangxi Emperor.","india":"Aurangzeb died in 1707 and the Mughal court fell into succession struggles; Farrukhsiyar now rules under the powerful Sayyid brothers. The Marathas are regrouping under Shahu, who appointed Balaji Vishwanath as his Peshwa in 1713.","changes":[{"name":"Spain","kind":"appeared","why":"When the last Habsburg king Charles II died childless in 1700, Europe fought over his throne. Louis XIV's grandson won it as Philip V, founding Spain's Bourbon line."},{"name":"Spanish Habsburg","kind":"vanished","why":"The Spanish Habsburg line ended with Charles II's death in 1700; the same lands passed to the Bourbon Philip V."},{"name":"Manchu Empire","kind":"grew","why":"The Kangxi Emperor took Taiwan in 1683 and brought the Khalkha Mongols under Qing rule at Dolon Nor in 1691."},{"name":"Ottoman Empire","kind":"shrank","why":"The map now shows Egypt, Algiers, Tunis and Tripoli separately. They were Ottoman in name but largely run by local governors and military elites."},{"name":"Netherlands","kind":"renamed","why":"Same state, renamed in the map data."},{"name":"Neterlands","kind":"renamed","why":"Same state, renamed in the map data."},{"name":"Portugal","kind":"grew","why":"Gold was discovered in Minas Gerais in Brazil in the 1690s, setting off a gold rush that drew settlers deep into the interior."},{"name":"United Kingdom","kind":"appeared","why":"The Acts of Union of 1707 joined the kingdoms of England and Scotland into the Kingdom of Great Britain."}],"spotlight":[{"name":"Maratha","fact":"Shivaji's Maratha state survived 27 years of war with Aurangzeb and would soon expand across much of India."},{"name":"Asante","fact":"Founded around 1700 by Osei Tutu, the Asante kingdom was united by the Golden Stool, said to have descended from the sky."},{"name":"Rupert's Land","fact":"This huge region drained by Hudson Bay was granted to the Hudson's Bay Company in 1670, which traded furs there for two centuries."}]},{"year":1783,"title":"Revolution and new empires","overview":"In 1783 Britain recognised the independence of the United States at the Treaty of Paris. Russia has become a vast empire stretching to the Pacific, and Qing China has reached its greatest size. In India, the Mughals have faded and new powers compete.","india":"Mughal emperor Shah Alam II rules little beyond Delhi. The Marathas are the strongest Indian power, the East India Company has ruled Bengal since Plassey (1757) and Buxar (1764), and Tipu Sultan of Mysore is fighting the British.","changes":[{"name":"Russian Empire","kind":"appeared","why":"Peter the Great proclaimed Russia an empire in 1721. Catherine the Great (r. 1762-1796) then expanded it further, annexing Crimea in 1783."},{"name":"Tsardom of Muscovy","kind":"vanished","why":"Muscovy became the Russian Empire when Peter the Great took the title of emperor in 1721."},{"name":"Qing Empire","kind":"appeared","why":"Same Qing dynasty as the Manchu Empire. Under the Qianlong Emperor it conquered the Dzungars and Xinjiang in the 1750s, reaching its greatest size."},{"name":"Manchu Empire","kind":"renamed","why":"Same state, renamed in the map data."},{"name":"Rupert's Land","kind":"appeared","why":"The fur-trading lands of the Hudson's Bay Company, chartered in 1670. After Britain took New France in 1763, Britain held almost all of northern North America."},{"name":"Spain","kind":"grew","why":"Spain received Louisiana from France in 1762-63, and at the 1783 Treaty of Paris it regained Florida from Britain."},{"name":"Portugal","kind":"grew","why":"Brazil is now counted with Portugal. The 1750 Treaty of Madrid also recognised Portuguese claims deep into the Amazon."},{"name":"Mughal Empire","kind":"vanished","why":"After Nader Shah sacked Delhi in 1739 and the Marathas and British rose, the Mughal emperor became a figurehead ruling only around Delhi."}],"spotlight":[{"name":"Mysore","fact":"Hyder Ali and his son Tipu Sultan used iron-cased war rockets against the British, inspiring later British rocket designs."},{"name":"United States of America","fact":"Its independence was recognised by Britain in the Treaty of Paris, signed on 3 September 1783."},{"name":"Travancore","fact":"King Marthanda Varma defeated the Dutch East India Company at the Battle of Colachel in 1741."}]},{"year":1800,"title":"The Company and the Sikhs","overview":"At the turn of the century Napoleon is rising in France, which has set up sister republics such as the Batavian and Helvetic Republics. Poland has been wiped off the map by its neighbours, and in India the British East India Company is the rising power.","india":"In 1799 the British killed Tipu Sultan at Seringapatam and restored the Wodeyars to a smaller Mysore. The Nizam of Hyderabad has signed a subsidiary alliance with the Company, and in Punjab, Ranjit Singh took Lahore in 1799.","changes":[{"name":"Viceroyalty of the Río de la Plata","kind":"appeared","why":"Spain created this viceroyalty in 1776, with its capital at Buenos Aires, to defend the region against the Portuguese and British."},{"name":"Poland","kind":"vanished","why":"Russia, Prussia and Austria divided Poland in three partitions (1772, 1793 and 1795), erasing it from the map for 123 years."},{"name":"Sikhs","kind":"appeared","why":"Ranjit Singh united the Sikh confederacies (misls), captured Lahore in 1799 and was proclaimed Maharaja of Punjab in 1801."},{"name":"Lahore","kind":"vanished","why":"Ranjit Singh captured Lahore in July 1799, making it the capital of his growing Sikh state."},{"name":"Rajputs","kind":"appeared","why":"The Rajput states of Rajasthan, such as Jaipur, Jodhpur and Udaipur, kept their own rulers but often paid tribute to the Marathas."},{"name":"Bundelkhand","kind":"appeared","why":"The Bundela Rajput lands in central India, once led by Chhatrasal, were now divided among smaller chiefs and Maratha allies."}],"spotlight":[{"name":"Nizam's Dominions","fact":"Founded by Asaf Jah I in 1724, Hyderabad became India's largest princely state and its Nizams were among the richest people in the world."},{"name":"Sikhs","fact":"Ranjit Singh later covered the Harmandir Sahib in Amritsar with gold, which is why it is known as the Golden Temple."},{"name":"Batavian Republic","fact":"Set up in 1795 when French revolutionary armies took the Netherlands, it was a French satellite state."}]},{"year":1815,"title":"After Napoleon","overview":"Napoleon was finally defeated at Waterloo in June 1815, and the Congress of Vienna redrew Europe's borders. Spain's American empire is in revolt, the United States has doubled in size, and Britain is the leading power at sea and in India.","india":"The East India Company is now the dominant power. It defeated the Marathas in 1803-05 and took Delhi, and the Anglo-Nepalese War (1814-16) is fixing Nepal's borders. Ranjit Singh still rules the Sikh kingdom of Punjab.","changes":[{"name":"Qing Empire","kind":"renamed","why":"Same state, renamed in the map data."},{"name":"Manchu Empire","kind":"renamed","why":"Same state, renamed in the map data."},{"name":"United States","kind":"appeared","why":"Same country as before, renamed in the data. It doubled in size when it bought Louisiana from Napoleon's France in 1803."},{"name":"Canada","kind":"appeared","why":"British North America, including Upper and Lower Canada and the Hudson's Bay lands, is now drawn as one. It fought off American invasions in the War of 1812."},{"name":"Rupert's Land","kind":"vanished","why":"The Hudson's Bay Company lands are now drawn as part of Canada in the map data."},{"name":"United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland","kind":"appeared","why":"The Acts of Union 1800 joined Ireland to Great Britain, creating this kingdom from 1 January 1801."},{"name":"France","kind":"shrank","why":"Napoleon's defeat, finally at Waterloo in 1815, returned France roughly to its 1790 borders, and it had sold Louisiana to the US in 1803."},{"name":"Denmark-Norway","kind":"vanished","why":"Denmark sided with Napoleon and lost. In the Treaty of Kiel (1814) it gave Norway to Sweden."},{"name":"Viceroyalty of the Río de la Plata","kind":"vanished","why":"Buenos Aires overthrew Spanish rule in the May Revolution of 1810, and the United Provinces declared independence in 1816."}],"spotlight":[{"name":"Kingdom of Hawaii","fact":"Kamehameha I completed the unification of the Hawaiian Islands in 1810."},{"name":"Sweden–Norway","fact":"Norway briefly declared independence in 1814 but accepted a union with Sweden that lasted until 1905."},{"name":"Sikkim (Indian princely state)","fact":"This small Himalayan kingdom was ruled by the Chogyal dynasty and became the Indian state of Sikkim in 1975."}]},{"year":1878,"title":"High noon of empire","overview":"Latin America has won its independence, Italy and Germany have unified, and Japan is modernising under the Meiji emperor. The United States now stretches from coast to coast, and European empires are about to scramble for Africa.","india":"After the revolt of 1857, the British Crown took over from the East India Company in 1858, and Queen Victoria was proclaimed Empress of India at the Delhi Durbar of 1877. The Great Famine of 1876-78 killed millions in the south and west.","changes":[{"name":"Spain","kind":"shrank","why":"Spain lost almost all its American mainland empire in the wars of independence led by Simon Bolivar, Jose de San Martin and others, ending after Ayacucho in 1824."},{"name":"United States of America","kind":"appeared","why":"Same country, renamed in the data. It grew hugely by annexing Texas (1845), Oregon (1846), the Mexican Cession (1848) and buying Alaska (1867)."},{"name":"United States","kind":"renamed","why":"Same state, renamed in the map data."},{"name":"Kingdom of Brazil","kind":"appeared","why":"Brazil declared independence from Portugal in 1822 under Pedro I, as an empire. In 1878 it was ruled by Emperor Pedro II."},{"name":"Portugal","kind":"shrank","why":"Portugal lost Brazil, by far its largest territory, when Brazil declared independence in 1822."},{"name":"Western Australia (UK)","kind":"appeared","why":"The Swan River Colony was founded in 1829 and grew into the colony of Western Australia, covering a third of the continent."},{"name":"Maratha Confederacy","kind":"vanished","why":"The British defeated the Marathas in the Third Anglo-Maratha War (1817-18), when Peshwa Baji Rao II surrendered and his lands were annexed."}],"spotlight":[{"name":"British Raj","fact":"After 1858, India was governed by a Viceroy in Calcutta answering to the Secretary of State for India in London."},{"name":"Imperial Japan","fact":"The Meiji Restoration of 1868 turned Japan from a feudal shogunate into an industrial power in just a few decades."},{"name":"Germany","fact":"Germany was unified under Prussia's leadership in 1871, with Wilhelm I proclaimed emperor in the Hall of Mirrors at Versailles."}]},{"year":1880,"title":"Wars on the frontiers","overview":"Just two years on, the big picture is similar, but frontier wars are reshaping the edges. Argentina has pushed south into Patagonia, and Britain is fighting in Afghanistan and southern Africa.","india":"The Second Anglo-Afghan War (1878-80) ended with the British defeat at Maiwand and victory at Kandahar in 1880; Abdur Rahman Khan became emir and Afghanistan stayed a buffer state. Lord Ripon became Viceroy in 1880.","changes":[{"name":"Argentina","kind":"grew","why":"In the Conquest of the Desert (1878-84), General Julio Roca led campaigns against Indigenous peoples of the Pampas and Patagonia, seizing their lands."},{"name":"Pampas cultures","kind":"vanished","why":"The Mapuche, Ranquel and other peoples of the Pampas were defeated and displaced by Argentina's Conquest of the Desert."},{"name":"Patagonian shellfish and marine mammal hunters","kind":"vanished","why":"Argentina and Chile claimed Patagonia, and its Indigenous peoples lost their lands as settlers and armies moved south."},{"name":"Sultanate of Zanzibar","kind":"renamed","why":"Same state, renamed in the map data."},{"name":"Sultinate of Zanzibar","kind":"renamed","why":"Same state, renamed in the map data."},{"name":"Māori","kind":"renamed","why":"Same state, renamed in the map data."},{"name":"M?ori","kind":"renamed","why":"Same state, renamed in the map data."}],"spotlight":[{"name":"Afghanistan","fact":"Abdur Rahman Khan, emir from 1880, was nicknamed the 'Iron Emir' for his harsh centralising rule."},{"name":"Sultanate of Zanzibar","fact":"Its rulers grew rich on cloves and on the East African slave trade, which Britain pressured them to end in 1873."},{"name":"Kingdom of Hawaii","fact":"King Kalakaua, who reigned from 1874, was the first reigning monarch to travel around the world, in 1881."}]},{"year":1900,"title":"The scramble is done","overview":"By 1900 almost all of Africa has been carved up by European powers after the Berlin Conference of 1884-85, and the Ottoman Empire has lost most of the Balkans. Australia's colonies are about to federate on 1 January 1901.","india":"Lord Curzon is Viceroy (1899-1905), and western India is suffering a terrible famine in 1899-1900. The Indian National Congress, founded in 1885, is slowly growing into a voice for self-rule.","changes":[{"name":"Kingdom of Brazil","kind":"vanished","why":"Brazil's monarchy was overthrown on 15 November 1889, and Emperor Pedro II went into exile. Brazil became a republic."},{"name":"United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland","kind":"grew","why":"Britain gained huge areas in the Scramble for Africa, and this map now draws the Australian colonies under the British label as well."},{"name":"Ottoman Empire","kind":"shrank","why":"After the war with Russia, the 1878 Congress of Berlin made Serbia, Romania and Montenegro independent, Bulgaria autonomous, and let Austria occupy Bosnia."},{"name":"Egypt","kind":"appeared","why":"Egypt was still nominally Ottoman, but Britain occupied it in 1882 and ran its affairs. With Britain it also reconquered Sudan in 1898."}],"spotlight":[{"name":"Rapa Nui","fact":"Easter Island, famous for its giant moai statues, was annexed by Chile in 1888."},{"name":"Afghanistan","fact":"The Durand Line, agreed in 1893, set the border between Afghanistan and British India."},{"name":"Second Samori Empire","fact":"Samori Ture resisted French conquest in West Africa for nearly two decades before he was captured in 1898."}]},{"year":1914,"title":"On the eve of the Great War","overview":"In 1914 Europe's empires cover much of the globe, and in August the First World War begins. China has become a republic after the fall of the Qing, and Britain's dominions such as Australia run their own affairs.","india":"King George V's Delhi Durbar of 1911 announced moving the capital from Calcutta to Delhi. Over a million Indian soldiers will serve overseas in the First World War, and Gandhi returns from South Africa in January 1915.","changes":[{"name":"Russian Empire","kind":"renamed","why":"Same state, renamed in the map data."},{"name":"Russia","kind":"renamed","why":"Same state, renamed in the map data."},{"name":"United States","kind":"renamed","why":"Same state, renamed in the map data."},{"name":"United States of America","kind":"renamed","why":"Same state, renamed in the map data."},{"name":"France","kind":"grew","why":"France built a vast African empire, forming French West Africa (1895) and French Equatorial Africa (1910), taking Madagascar (1896) and a protectorate over Morocco (1912)."},{"name":"Brazil","kind":"appeared","why":"Brazil has been a republic since the monarchy was overthrown in 1889."},{"name":"Australia","kind":"appeared","why":"The six British colonies federated into the Commonwealth of Australia on 1 January 1901."},{"name":"Manchu Empire","kind":"shrank","why":"The Xinhai Revolution of 1911 ended the Qing dynasty, and the last emperor Puyi abdicated in 1912. Mongolia and Tibet broke away."},{"name":"Belgium","kind":"grew","why":"Belgium annexed the Congo Free State in 1908, taking it from King Leopold II after outrage over abuses there."}],"spotlight":[{"name":"Tibet","fact":"The 13th Dalai Lama expelled Chinese troops after the fall of the Qing and declared Tibet independent in 1913."},{"name":"Abyssinia","fact":"Ethiopia stayed free by defeating Italy at the Battle of Adwa in 1896, one of the few African states never colonised."},{"name":"Mongolia","fact":"Mongolia declared independence in 1911 under the Bogd Khan, a Buddhist religious leader."}]},{"year":1920,"title":"After the war to end all wars","overview":"The First World War toppled four empires: Russian, German, Austro-Hungarian and Ottoman. New states like Poland, Czechoslovakia and the Baltic republics appear, and the Bolsheviks are winning Russia's civil war.","india":"The Jallianwala Bagh massacre in Amritsar on 13 April 1919 shocked India. In 1920 Gandhi launched the Non-Cooperation Movement, turning the Congress into a mass movement for self-rule.","changes":[{"name":"Russia","kind":"vanished","why":"The Bolsheviks under Lenin seized power in October 1917 and won the civil war. The Soviet Union was formally founded in December 1922."},{"name":"USSR","kind":"appeared","why":"The Bolshevik state that replaced the Russian Empire; it was formally named the Union of Soviet Socialist Republics in December 1922."},{"name":"Manchu Empire","kind":"vanished","why":"The Qing dynasty ended in 1912, and China became a republic."},{"name":"Chinese Warlords","kind":"appeared","why":"After President Yuan Shikai died in 1916, China split among regional military leaders, the start of the warlord era."},{"name":"Ottoman Empire","kind":"vanished","why":"The Ottomans lost the war and their Arab lands. The Treaty of Sevres (1920) left them a small rump state in Anatolia."},{"name":"Sudan","kind":"renamed","why":"Same state, renamed in the map data."},{"name":"Union of South Africa","kind":"renamed","why":"Same state, renamed in the map data."}],"spotlight":[{"name":"Poland","fact":"Poland was reborn as an independent state in November 1918 after 123 years off the map."},{"name":"Czechoslovakia","fact":"Founded in 1918, it combined Czechs and Slovaks and was led by its first president, Tomas Masaryk."},{"name":"Hejaz","fact":"The Kingdom of Hejaz, ruled by Sharif Hussein of Mecca, launched the Arab Revolt against the Ottomans in 1916."}]},{"year":1930,"title":"Between the wars","overview":"The postwar map has settled. Turkey is a new republic under Mustafa Kemal, the Soviet Union has formed, and the world is entering the Great Depression after the Wall Street crash of 1929.","india":"On 26 January 1930 the Congress celebrated Purna Swaraj (complete independence) day, and on 12 March Gandhi began the Salt March to Dandi, defying the British salt tax.","changes":[{"name":"White Russia","kind":"renamed","why":"Same state, renamed in the map data."},{"name":"USSR","kind":"renamed","why":"Same state, renamed in the map data."},{"name":"Republic of Turkey","kind":"appeared","why":"Mustafa Kemal (Ataturk) led the Turkish War of Independence and proclaimed the Republic of Turkey on 29 October 1923."},{"name":"Ottoman Sultanate","kind":"vanished","why":"The sultanate was abolished on 1 November 1922, and the last sultan, Mehmed VI, left Istanbul."},{"name":"Ukraine","kind":"vanished","why":"Ukraine became the Ukrainian SSR, a founding republic of the Soviet Union in 1922."}],"spotlight":[{"name":"Danzig","fact":"A Free City under League of Nations protection from 1920, it was a flashpoint between Germany and Poland."},{"name":"Afghanistan","fact":"Afghanistan gained full control of its foreign affairs from Britain in 1919 after the Third Anglo-Afghan War."},{"name":"Tibet","fact":"The 13th Dalai Lama ruled Tibet as an effectively independent state until his death in 1933."}]},{"year":1938,"title":"Storm clouds gather","overview":"By 1938 Italy has conquered Ethiopia, Japan has invaded China, and Germany has annexed Austria. Britain and France still rule huge colonial empires, while the Soviet Union is in the grip of Stalin's purges.","india":"Under the Government of India Act 1935, elections were held in 1937 and Congress formed governments in most provinces. Burma was separated from British India on 1 April 1937.","changes":[{"name":"White Russia","kind":"renamed","why":"Same state, renamed in the map data."},{"name":"USSR","kind":"renamed","why":"Same state, renamed in the map data."},{"name":"Chinese Warlords","kind":"renamed","why":"Same state, renamed in the map data."},{"name":"Chinese warlords","kind":"renamed","why":"Same state, renamed in the map data."},{"name":"Saudi Arabia","kind":"appeared","why":"Ibn Saud conquered the Hejaz in 1925 and united it with Nejd, proclaiming the Kingdom of Saudi Arabia on 23 September 1932. Oil was struck in 1938."}],"spotlight":[{"name":"Ethiopia (Italy)","fact":"Mussolini's Italy invaded Ethiopia in 1935, and Emperor Haile Selassie appealed in vain to the League of Nations."},{"name":"Ireland","fact":"A new constitution in 1937 renamed the Irish Free State as Ireland (Eire)."},{"name":"Siam","fact":"Siam, never colonised, changed its name to Thailand in 1939."}]},{"year":1945,"title":"The war ends, empires crack","overview":"The Second World War ended in 1945. Germany and Korea are divided into occupation zones, the United Nations is founded, and colonies across Asia are demanding freedom. The map already shows several new states born in the following years.","india":"The map already shows the 1947 borders. On 15 August 1947 British rule ended and the subcontinent was partitioned into India and Pakistan, with millions displaced and many killed. East Pakistan later became Bangladesh in 1971.","changes":[{"name":"Antarctica","kind":"appeared","why":"Antarctica now appears on the map. Several countries claimed slices of it, claims later frozen by the Antarctic Treaty of 1959."},{"name":"USA","kind":"renamed","why":"Same state, renamed in the map data."},{"name":"United States","kind":"renamed","why":"Same state, renamed in the map data."},{"name":"China","kind":"appeared","why":"Chiang Kai-shek's Nationalist government regained control of much of China after Japan's surrender in 1945, but civil war with Mao's Communists soon resumed."},{"name":"Chinese warlords","kind":"vanished","why":"The warlord era faded as the Nationalists and then the war against Japan brought China under more central control."},{"name":"India","kind":"appeared","why":"India became independent on 15 August 1947, with Jawaharlal Nehru as its first Prime Minister."},{"name":"Zaire","kind":"renamed","why":"Same state, renamed in the map data."},{"name":"Union of South Africa","kind":"renamed","why":"Same state, renamed in the map data."}],"spotlight":[{"name":"Pakistan","fact":"Pakistan was created in 1947 under Muhammad Ali Jinnah, in two parts separated by about 1,600 km of India."},{"name":"Indonesia","fact":"Sukarno declared Indonesia's independence from the Dutch on 17 August 1945."},{"name":"Korea (USSR)","fact":"Korea was split at the 38th parallel in 1945, with Soviet forces in the north and US forces in the south."}]},{"year":1960,"title":"The year of Africa","overview":"Empires are dissolving. Britain and France have lost most of their colonies, and in 1960 alone 17 African countries won independence. The Cold War divides the world between the US and Soviet blocs, with Germany and Korea split in two.","india":"India has been a republic since 26 January 1950, led by Prime Minister Nehru. States were redrawn on linguistic lines from 1956, and in 1960 Bombay State split into Maharashtra and Gujarat. Goa is still Portuguese until December 1961.","changes":[{"name":"United Kingdom","kind":"shrank","why":"Britain gave up India (1947), Burma and Sri Lanka (1948), Ghana and Malaya (1957), and Nigeria (October 1960), with more to follow."},{"name":"France","kind":"shrank","why":"France left Indochina after Dien Bien Phu (1954), Morocco and Tunisia became independent in 1956, and much of French Africa became independent in 1960."},{"name":"USA","kind":"renamed","why":"Same state, renamed in the map data."},{"name":"United States","kind":"renamed","why":"Same state, renamed in the map data."},{"name":"China","kind":"grew","why":"Mao Zedong proclaimed the People's Republic on 1 October 1949 and brought Xinjiang and Manchuria firmly under Beijing's control."},{"name":"Sudan","kind":"appeared","why":"Sudan became independent from Britain and Egypt on 1 January 1956."},{"name":"Algeria","kind":"appeared","why":"Algeria was fighting a bitter war of independence against France (1954-62) and won independence in July 1962."}],"spotlight":[{"name":"Ghana","fact":"Led by Kwame Nkrumah, it became independent on 6 March 1957, one of the first sub-Saharan African colonies to do so."},{"name":"East Germany","fact":"The Berlin Wall would be built here in August 1961 to stop people fleeing to the West."},{"name":"Korea, Republic of","fact":"The Korean War (1950-53) ended in an armistice, not a peace treaty, so the two Koreas remain technically at war."}]},{"year":1994,"title":"The Soviet Union breaks up","overview":"The Cold War is over. The Berlin Wall fell in 1989, Germany reunited in 1990, and the Soviet Union dissolved in December 1991 into 15 countries. Yugoslavia broke apart in war, while Czechoslovakia split peacefully.","india":"India opened up its economy in 1991 under Prime Minister P. V. Narasimha Rao and Finance Minister Manmohan Singh, cutting licences and tariffs and starting decades of faster growth.","changes":[{"name":"USSR","kind":"vanished","why":"Mikhail Gorbachev's reforms loosened Moscow's grip, a failed coup in August 1991 sped the collapse, and the USSR was dissolved on 26 December 1991."},{"name":"Russia","kind":"appeared","why":"Russia, led by Boris Yeltsin, became the successor state of the Soviet Union and took over its UN Security Council seat."},{"name":"Kazakhstan","kind":"appeared","why":"Kazakhstan declared independence on 16 December 1991, the last Soviet republic to do so."},{"name":"Ukraine","kind":"appeared","why":"Ukraine declared independence on 24 August 1991, confirmed by more than 90 percent of voters in a referendum that December."},{"name":"Turkmenistan","kind":"appeared","why":"Turkmenistan became independent in October 1991 as the Soviet Union collapsed."},{"name":"Uzbekistan","kind":"appeared","why":"Uzbekistan declared independence in 1991 as the Soviet Union collapsed."},{"name":"Germany","kind":"appeared","why":"East and West Germany reunited on 3 October 1990, less than a year after the fall of the Berlin Wall."},{"name":"Yugoslavia","kind":"vanished","why":"Slovenia and Croatia declared independence in 1991, followed by Macedonia and Bosnia, sparking wars including the Bosnian War of 1992-95."},{"name":"Tibet","kind":"vanished","why":"Tibet was taken over by the People's Republic of China in 1950-51 and became the Tibet Autonomous Region in 1965."}],"spotlight":[{"name":"Czech Republic","fact":"Czechoslovakia split peacefully into the Czech Republic and Slovakia on 1 January 1993, the 'Velvet Divorce'."},{"name":"Bangladesh","fact":"Bangladesh won independence from Pakistan in 1971 after a war in which India intervened."},{"name":"Russia","fact":"Even after losing the other Soviet republics, Russia remains the largest country in the world, spanning 11 time zones."}]},{"year":2000,"title":"A new millennium","overview":"The borders of 2000 look much like those of 1994. Hong Kong returned to China in 1997 and Macau in 1999, and East Timor voted for independence from Indonesia in 1999. The internet is spreading fast.","india":"India tested nuclear weapons at Pokhran in May 1998, and fought the Kargil War with Pakistan in 1999. In 2000 its population passed one billion, and three new states, Chhattisgarh, Uttarakhand and Jharkhand, were formed in November.","changes":[],"spotlight":[{"name":"China","fact":"Hong Kong returned to Chinese rule on 1 July 1997 under the 'one country, two systems' formula."},{"name":"Zaire","fact":"The country was renamed the Democratic Republic of the Congo in 1997, after Mobutu Sese Seko was overthrown."},{"name":"India","fact":"India's population passed one billion in May 2000."}]},{"year":2010,"title":"Today's world map","overview":"The world of 2010 has nearly 200 countries. Since 2000, East Timor (2002) and Montenegro (2006) became independent, Kosovo declared independence in 2008, and China and India are rising fast as economic powers.","india":"India is one of the world's fastest-growing major economies. Chandrayaan-1 found evidence of water on the Moon in 2009, and Delhi hosted the Commonwealth Games in October 2010.","changes":[],"spotlight":[{"name":"Sudan","fact":"In a January 2011 referendum, South Sudan voted to secede, becoming the world's newest country that July."},{"name":"Montenegro","fact":"Montenegro voted to leave its union with Serbia in May 2006 by a margin of just over the 55 percent required."},{"name":"India","fact":"India is the world's largest democracy; its 2009 general election had over 700 million eligible voters."}]}];
